WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.340
- The African American Studies Department

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.180
at Yale's Endeavors Series.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.820
This is like our colloquium

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.310
where we learn and wander together.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:16.130
This being the second on an
anthropology of abolition,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:18.740
also an anthropology of liberation,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.010
is here with a tremendous
group of scholars

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.360
who I could not be more excited
to be you learning from.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.150
The housekeeping is for those of you

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.350
who are not amongst the panel,

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.680
I will encourage you to
turn your cameras off.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.380
The reason I'm encouraging
you to turn your cameras off

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.370
is because we are recording this

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.660
and we will eventually
be putting this up online

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:41.610
is the ideal.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.550
And if you do not want to be online

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.420
or you do not out want
to sign the paperwork,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.863
cameras off is deeply preferred.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.750
The other bit is,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.850
if you would go ahead and put
your questions in the chat,

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.300
keep your mics on mute.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.440
And Aimee will,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.370
during the Q and A be able to facilitate

00:00:58.370 --> 00:00:59.850
those questions for you.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.360
Please go ahead and put them in the chat,

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.510
populate them in there.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.810
I have a feeling we will
have lots of question

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:08.490
for all the tremendous wisdom
we are about to receive.

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:11.720
So with that, in this spring of 2022,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:14.250
it is my great pleasure to introduce

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.890
the trend setting, trail blazing,

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.690
pinnacle of the apotheosis of
anthropological excellence,

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.480
Aimee Cox, to introduce
the rest of this panel.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.360
Thank you all so very much.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.990
And again, welcome to African
American Studies Endeavors.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.390
- Ah, Phil, thank you so much.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.810
I knew you would coming hot.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:36.050
So, even a few minutes late,
it's like Phil comes in hot.

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.890
So thank you so much for
starting us off so vibrantly,

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.600
I also wanna thank Vanessa Epps

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.430
who's our senior administrative assistant

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.770
in African American
Studies for all the work

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.570
that Vanessa did to organize
all the details around this

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.603
Endeavors Series.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.040
So wonderful to see all of you here

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.740
and more people are still arriving

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.200
or at least to see you
in your Zoom squares,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.510
but I'm not at all surprised

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.980
given who we are in
conversation with this morning

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.780
into this afternoon.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:05.320
But first, I want to
start off by expressing

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.840
how profoundly fortifying,

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.470
even before we have
begun our conversation,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.093
the anticipation of a
conversation with you,

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:18.130
Deb, Savannah and Christen is,

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:20.910
because I not only have
deep respect for your work

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.590
but I also care deeply about you.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.180
And I do think that maybe
like two and a half years ago

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.610
I may have been a little hesitant
to express that sentiment

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.820
in an academic context,
to be completely honest,

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:34.530
but much has changed in the
past two years and a half.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.620
And it has become clear to me at least

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.010
that operating as if care and connection

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.690
are outside of our intellectual concern

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:44.630
or irrelevant to our scholarship,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.180
constitutes a persistent,
if subtle, type of violence.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.230
And I would add care and
connection are essential

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:55.050
for how we think about
abolition and move towards it.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.070
So this is a conversation that
we have been having already

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:01.610
in many ways across time,
phases in our careers,

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.220
several different institutions

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:06.500
and our own shifting
relationship to the academy,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.440
our research and the
discipline of anthropology.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.300
But this is now a conversation
that takes place in...

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.337
And I'm doing this "these times."

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.220
Over the past two and a half
years, almost three years,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.360
we have heard the phrase "in
these times" more than ever,

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.470
the times that people are
referring to could range from

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:30.530
the ongoing global pandemic and
the lives taken in its wake,

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.700
global protests against anti-Blackness

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:37.350
and structural racism, threats
to an idealized democracy,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.540
the disruption of identities
constructed on strict binaries

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:42.740
and scales of comparison,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.630
and the realization that former
modes of supposed stability

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:50.360
through financial security,
social respectability

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:54.183
and the accumulation of
credentials are no longer viable.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.300
I will pause here, but the
list could go on as you know,

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.420
but these are the times we ourselves in,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.680
which also means,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.550
these are the times the
discipline of anthropology

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:08.973
and the concept, an act of
abolition move within and around.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.450
The academic iteration of anthropology

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.560
is also currently confronting a long...

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.980
Let me be clear, a long history

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.480
of sexual violence that is just one...

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.520
Let me be clear,

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:25.270
just one branch on the
deeply rooted tree of power

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:27.440
held by singular gatekeepers

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.360
who not only violate
bodies and boundaries,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.860
but determine who gets
hired, mentored, awarded,

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.873
and which scholarship is
legitimized as knowledge.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.840
So, when I shared the idea
for today's conversation

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:42.830
with a close friend of mine

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:45.180
who happens to be a literary scholar,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.417
their response was, and I quote,

00:04:47.417 --> 00:04:50.477
"You mean to say the
abolition of anthropology,

00:04:50.477 --> 00:04:53.677
"not an anthropology of abolition, right?

00:04:53.677 --> 00:04:57.367
"Because the abolition of
anthropology is the only way

00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:00.477
"those two words can be
reconciled in the same breath."

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.880
And my response was,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.180
who said, we should be
seeking reconciliation.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:06.923
I don't know,

00:05:06.923 --> 00:05:09.353
can anthropology serve
the ideals of abolition?

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:12.250
And then what about
that slash in the title,

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.660
the slash in the title between
abolition and liberation,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.250
are they truly an and/or,

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.983
or have I too readily
assumed a direct link?

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:24.300
Can we get specific about our terms

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:27.150
so that they inspire
action and accountability,

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:30.913
and not just the ongoing loop
of our perpetual theorizing?

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.560
I don't have easy answers,
I doubt any of us do,

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.770
but we do have the gift of
three courageous thinkers

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.000
to wade in these choppy waters with us.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.810
So, this is how we will proceed today.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.440
I'll introduce our three speakers

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.960
and then each will offer opening remarks,

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.460
starting with Dr. Shange.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.742
Can I use your first names, do you mind?

00:05:51.742 --> 00:05:54.530
(laughs) Starting with Savannah,

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:57.850
moving to Christen and then moving to Deb.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.710
I'll moderate the conversation
for about 20 to 25 minutes

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.020
before opening up to your
questions and comments

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.023
so that we can have a full
dialogue in this space.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:08.880
So, Savannah Shange,

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.170
who gets a big hand clap for joining us

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.500
at 5:30 in the morning.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.660
Should I reveal where you are,

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:16.810
so we don't feel so
sorry for you (laughs)?

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.180
So, Savannah is an Assistant
Professor of Anthropology

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:23.810
at UC Santa Cruz,

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.020
and also serves as principal faculty

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:28.480
in Critical Race and Ethnic Studies.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.380
She earned a PhD in Africana studies

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.877
in education from the
University of Pennsylvania.

00:06:33.877 --> 00:06:36.210
"MAT," is that a master's in education,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.370
masters from Tufts University

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.380
and a BFA in experimental theater

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.180
from Tisch School Of The Arts at NYU.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.217
Her first brilliant book,

00:06:45.217 --> 00:06:48.830
"Progressive Dystopia:
Abolition, AntiBlackness,

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.850
and Schooling in San Francisco,"

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:53.440
published by Duke in 2019,

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.880
is an ethnography of
the afterlife of slavery

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.840
as lived in the Bay Area.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.810
And also, and Savannah,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.770
I hope you've added this to your bio.

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.850
It's also the winner of the
prestigious Bateson Book Prize,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:07.690
that's awarded through the society

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.820
for cultural anthropology.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:12.020
Savannah, we're so happy to have you here.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.090
We give you a virtual hand clap.

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.570
Christen A. Smith,

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.570
is Associate Professor of Anthropology

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.310
and African and African diaspora studies

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.720
and director of the Center
for Women's and Gender Studies

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.490
at the University of Texas at Austin.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:28.860
Her work focuses on the
immediate and long term impact

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:33.010
of policing on Black
communities in the Americas,

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.430
particularly on Black women.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:36.300
She's the author of

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.790
over 20 peer reviewed articles and essays,

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.487
multiple public commentaries
and a brilliant book,

00:07:41.487 --> 00:07:43.150
"Afro-Paradise:

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:46.040
Blackness, Violence, and
Performance in Brazil"

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.060
published in 2016,

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:49.700
which won an honorable mentioned

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.180
for the Errol Hill Award in 2017.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.740
Christen is also currently
completing three books.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.003
Is that true, Christen?

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.910
Christen is in the middle of
three brilliant book projects.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:02.950
Which I hope you'll talk about

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.180
as we get into our conversation.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:06.360
Deb A. Thomas,

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.580
Deborah A. Thomas is the
R. Jean Brownlee Professor

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:10.910
of Anthropology

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:14.270
and Director of the Center
for Experimental Ethnography

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.580
at the University of Pennsylvania.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:17.413
She's the author of

00:08:17.413 --> 00:08:20.317
"Political Life in the
Wake of the Plantation,"

00:08:20.317 --> 00:08:23.610
"Exceptional Violence"
and "Modern Blackness."

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.950
Thomas co-directed the
documentary films "Bad Friday"

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:28.890
and "Four Days in May."

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.350
And she is the co-curator

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.467
of a multimedia installation titled

00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:36.240
"Bearing Witness: Four
Days in West Kingston."

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.880
Prior to her life in the academy,

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:39.640
she was a beautiful...

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:40.900
Not prior to, still a dancer,

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:42.330
but she was a professional dancer

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:45.850
with the New York based Urban Bush Women.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.520
Can we give a virtual hand clap

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:51.640
to our esteemed panelists today?

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.350
So, I'm gonna open up,

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:54.450
with you all opening up

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.180
and you're opening remarks.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.530
And I want you to open up

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.010
in whatever way feels right to you.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.850
But if this is a helpful prompt,

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:05.960
I thought we might interrogate
some of the assumptions

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.930
that I might be making in
the terms that I'm using.

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:13.790
So, abolition is a word
that many of us use now,

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.720
I would say in past, maybe five years,

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.700
it's become more mainstreamed,
associated with policing

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.830
in the carceral state more specifically,

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.000
but then now it has this traffic

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:24.620
and people are attaching it

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:26.410
in the way that we do it in the academy.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:28.920
And there's this assumption
that we're all using it

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.180
or understand how we're using it.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:35.480
And I'm wondering how you
enter this conversation

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:38.580
and your relationship to
the concept of abolition.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.150
But then I thought,

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:43.930
am I also making an assumption
about anthropology, right?

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:46.430
Do you, in this moment, consider
yourself an anthropologist?

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.970
What is your relationship to the field?

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.770
Because your work is housed
in an anthropology department

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.910
and read by anthropologist or not,

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:58.380
how do you assess your relationship

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.580
to the discipline of anthropology?

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:03.100
And this could be within
and outside of the academy

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.350
and then how do we reconcile or not,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.940
referring to that question from my friend,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.540
the concept of abolition

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:15.540
and what we understand to be
the practice of anthropology?

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:19.860
Or you can just ignore that
and just whatever you wanna say

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.460
to open up is fine.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:23.550
But we'll start with Savannah.

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:25.813
Savannah, I'm gonna turn it over to you.

00:10:26.972 --> 00:10:27.805
- Thank you so much

00:10:27.805 --> 00:10:30.270
for that really beautiful
introduction, Aimee.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:32.540
And it's so wonderful to
be here with so many folks

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:36.130
who have literally shaped
my path to this moment.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.530
So, it's wonderful to be here with you,

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.803
Christen and Deb too.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:45.170
So yes, I feel like this past
year and a half has really,

00:10:48.890 --> 00:10:51.530
I think highlighted for
me the need to be clear

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.800
about what it means when we say abolition,

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:56.470
because I feel like 10 years ago,

00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:57.620
I feel like people who said it,

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.590
I was on the same page with them.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:02.740
And now, we're in a different moment.

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:05.650
And actually just recently I
finished working on a piece,

00:11:05.650 --> 00:11:08.650
trying to work through this very concern.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:10.850
So I think I'll share
maybe a couple pages of it

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:14.240
and then talk about how it
brings me back to this moment.

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.680
And so it starts,

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:20.670
do you remember maybe some
folks here, June and July, 2020

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.493
when in addition to our communities,

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.840
rising up in the streets,

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:30.400
our colleagues who were not in the streets

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:31.630
were rising up in our emails

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.710
to ask us to appear every six minutes.

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:35.600
You remember that, right?

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.047
And so what started as a series of,

00:11:37.047 --> 00:11:39.130
we had some really
generative conversations,

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:42.483
but then you're on the third
talk in the sixth week,

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:46.390
you start being kind of a
blank thought vending machine,

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.140
It's a PEZ dispenser
for bites of abolition.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:50.480
And I realize, I'm just gonna say,

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.000
I put myself in that position

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:56.470
by putting abolition in
a subtitle of my book,

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:59.950
not knowing this is where I
was going to be in this moment.

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:04.140
And so, I've been thinking
about what it means to use

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.360
the kind of (indistinct) logics

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.270
that come out of search
engines like Google, right?

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:12.450
In the ways we even
literally title our work.

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.810
Because then the same
engines of surveillance

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:18.310
and digital capture then serve

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:22.590
to lubricate certain kinds of pathways

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:26.760
to finding certain kinds of works

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.610
as an entryway to
something like abolition,

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.530
when there's other places,

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:32.247
namely the streets that you live on

00:12:32.247 --> 00:12:34.260
and the places that we work,

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.630
that are better entrance points,

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:37.940
at least I think more earnest ones.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.900
And so, I was asked in
one of these sessions,

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.020
how do you maintain faith in anthropology

00:12:43.020 --> 00:12:44.653
while doing abolitionist work?

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.290
And this was a really earnest question

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:52.290
from a fantastic and super
engaged graduate student.

00:12:53.240 --> 00:12:56.460
And I realized that I had...

00:12:56.460 --> 00:12:58.490
The fact that this was the question,

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.180
really let me know how off path I was

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.440
because embedded in the logic
of a question like this,

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:04.750
and this was a good faith question.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:05.930
This was not...

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:07.490
This is someone really trying to do

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:09.360
the best work they can imagine.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.880
There's a twin assumption that,

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:14.500
1, anthropology and abolition
are reconcilable and then,

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:15.950
2, my spiritual investments

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:18.060
include a academic discipline.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:19.000
That's wrong on both counts,

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:21.750
but it's really on me
for having set that up.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:22.683
And so,

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:27.960
I think that as someone
who for better or worse

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:31.460
has hung my hat on abolitionists
anthropology as a phrase,

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:35.210
I think I set up both the
audience in these kind of spaces

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:36.480
and myself with a okie-doke,

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:39.950
because monographs are
meant to be consumable.

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:41.750
They're bite-able, they're citable,

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:44.660
and abolition is supposed to be ignitable.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:46.569
These aren't the same kind of function.

00:13:46.569 --> 00:13:47.830
And I think it's important to remember

00:13:47.830 --> 00:13:50.060
that abolition is not a metaphor, right?

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:53.060
And that our notion of abolition,

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:56.360
it's essential that doesn't
get de-fanged and (indistinct)

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.290
within these kind of
self-evident boundaries

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:00.000
of a discipline or the academy.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.310
And so, to maintain faith

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:05.500
in anthropology

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:07.350
while doing abolitionist work

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:10.270
implies a temporality in
which, like Aimee said,

00:14:10.270 --> 00:14:13.240
the discipline is ongoing and abiding.

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:15.840
While abolition would
be a tactical modifier

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.650
of labor in the meantime.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:19.820
The desire to deserve to
conserve the discipline

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.760
in the face of impossible demands,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:25.660
harkens to what Ryan
Jobson that same year,

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.393
named the anthropological fix,

00:14:27.393 --> 00:14:30.020
Whether it's technological,
status, (indistinct).

00:14:31.141 --> 00:14:32.200
And Ryan reminds us

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:33.590
that rather than looking to the discipline

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:35.110
to bolster our faith,

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.677
quote, "The decisive action
require to resolve a crisis

00:14:37.677 --> 00:14:39.750
"in anthropology on the contrary

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:42.470
will not being confined to the
halls of convention centers

00:14:42.470 --> 00:14:43.920
or university departments."

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.790
And of course not to like
a Zoom webinar, right?

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:48.330
Because abolition is not a fix.

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.860
It's the reverse.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.330
And unfixing in the sense
of disrupting both the locus

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:54.833
and the function of captivity.

00:14:55.780 --> 00:14:57.380
Given the distance between

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:59.500
our discipline's liberal center of gravity

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:01.030
and the demands of abolition,

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:02.290
it becomes clear that the question

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:05.060
from this graduate student
had it all backwards, right?

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:06.560
Instead of wondering the reverse,

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:08.830
how do we maintain faith in abolition

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.817
while doing anthropological work?

00:15:10.817 --> 00:15:13.490
And so I couldn't think
of this at the moment,

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:14.623
this is kind of...

00:15:14.623 --> 00:15:16.010
I was thinking about this in response

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:19.560
to this really generative question.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.420
And for me, the answer is clear.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.060
Every empire falls

00:15:23.060 --> 00:15:24.880
and this little settler dumpster fire

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.670
that we find ourselves
in, is no different.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:29.410
The planet that we freight so much about

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.640
will be absolutely fine.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:33.900
The earth will heal itself
and regenerate life,

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:35.280
time and time again,

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.580
over the next billion years.

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:39.160
The question is about,

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:42.510
whether humans will be
around to bear witness?

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.050
And so, any student of history

00:15:44.050 --> 00:15:45.760
knows the structure of the United States

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.180
like any nation state is
also gonna change and morph

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:50.880
in one way or another in
the next few hundred years.

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.990
But most likely those
transformations will fortify

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.723
the fatal forces of white
supremacy and patriarchy.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.580
The question is how we intend

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.030
to both shape and survive
those transformations, right?

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.840
So in 2021, 23,

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.930
will we see the North American
corporate military structure

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:12.270
grow even more powerful in
the ruins of neoliberalism?

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:14.080
Or will we see autonomous communities

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:15.840
accountable to indigenous lands

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:18.300
towards whole large swaths of land?

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:19.373
Will it be of both?

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:25.510
And so I think the last
piece I'll share here

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:31.100
is about where ethnographic practice

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:34.320
and anthropological ways
of knowing fit in here.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:37.440
And I think that amidst all of the miras

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.090
that the person you were
talking about, Aimee was saying,

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:42.150
this has to go, right?

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.350
Amidst everything that
has to go into the bin,

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:46.660
let's think of it as a compost pile

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:48.920
that anthropology has to go into.

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:50.620
Which means it's gonna have to decompose

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:54.320
and something else could
potentially be fed from that.

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.780
And particularly there's parts
of the disciplinary toolkit

00:16:56.780 --> 00:16:59.010
that are really useful for world making,

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:01.570
listening deeply, bearing witness,

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:04.090
challenging the
inevitability of the state,

00:17:04.090 --> 00:17:07.533
building deep transnational
and cross diasphoric relations,

00:17:08.700 --> 00:17:12.150
in particular as surveillance
and policing and imprisonment

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.750
become globalized as
techniques of oppression,

00:17:14.750 --> 00:17:17.650
anthropology can help us
disrupt U.S. centrism,

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.263
while cultivating thicker solidarities

00:17:19.263 --> 00:17:22.710
that function as political pathways.

00:17:22.710 --> 00:17:25.130
And so for me abolition is about

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:26.890
attending to the break,

00:17:26.890 --> 00:17:30.890
stretching open the cracks
in captivities infrastructure

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:32.730
and ethnographic practice is a technique

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:35.750
that can help us attend to
life as it is in this moment,

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:37.390
one that has the potential to attune us

00:17:37.390 --> 00:17:39.893
to world making as it's
happening everywhere.

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:41.920
And so...

00:17:43.648 --> 00:17:45.248
Aimee, do I have another minute.

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:48.050
- Please, go ahead.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:48.883
- Okay.

00:17:49.808 --> 00:17:51.610
So was anybody at one of the first

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:53.700
Critical Resistance
Conferences that's here,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.550
feel free to say in the chat,

00:17:55.550 --> 00:17:58.230
do y'all remember, there
was one at maybe...

00:17:58.230 --> 00:17:59.430
I was at the one at Columbia,

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:01.250
but there's one before
that maybe at Riverside.

00:18:01.250 --> 00:18:05.633
And so, at these initial CR
Conferences in like 97 and 99,

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.180
correct me on the years,

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:10.380
when folks said abolition,

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:12.480
it was understood as a
very direct reference

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:15.070
to destruction of the prison
industrial complex, right?

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:17.170
Policing wasn't even that central

00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:18.140
to the table at that moment.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.390
It was really specific about
the prison-industrial complex,

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.620
what some people would now
call mass incarceration,

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:25.640
but that's not the call,

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.720
the call is to end all incarceration,

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:29.940
not justify certain people's captivity

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:32.610
in order to free others.

00:18:32.610 --> 00:18:33.667
And of course the theory
of prison abolition

00:18:33.667 --> 00:18:36.500
was not born at that
conference or any conference

00:18:36.500 --> 00:18:38.470
in the history of anti-carceral thought

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:42.870
goes back depending on
how you wanna map it from,

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:44.570
Du Bois, from...

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:47.010
There's lots of ways to think about it.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.310
George James has a great
historiography of that,

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:52.300
but it wasn't until the 2020 uprisings

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:54.090
that the abolitionist proposition

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:56.000
appears to mainstream U.S. consciousness

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.317
with the (indistinct) demand, right.

00:18:57.317 --> 00:18:58.820
"Defund the Police."

00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:00.890
And there was so much hostility

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:03.360
and patronizing happening
in response to that,

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:05.080
that folks literally like Mariame Kaba

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:07.780
had to literally write a oped that said,

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:10.880
yes, we mean literally abolish the police.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:12.660
And so in the meantime,

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.940
there's been this rebranding
and bad faith efforts

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:18.600
to co-opt abolition as anything but,

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:22.100
and I have plenty of examples
of that, that I won't share.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:23.980
But for me, what I've
been thinking about is

00:19:23.980 --> 00:19:26.320
what are the different modes of abolition.

00:19:26.320 --> 00:19:30.290
So even among those who are
in the wheelhouse of okay,

00:19:30.290 --> 00:19:32.893
ending captivity and all of
the things that could cause it,

00:19:32.893 --> 00:19:36.830
there's still a lot of
generative diversity

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:38.030
within that frame.

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:40.400
And so I've been thinking about abolition

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:43.650
as five years of a moving
stick shift vehicle,

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:46.210
shout-out to everybody
who can drive manual.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:49.670
using Chela Sandoval's theory
of differential consciousness

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:51.440
that works through a gear.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:53.320
And so those gears would be,

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:56.030
we might think of as
abolition as rupture, right.

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:58.353
Which is changing the
conditions of captivity.

00:19:59.410 --> 00:20:01.590
Rupturing the common sense
that produces captivity.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:05.230
So someone like Mariame Kaba
and the Insight Collective.

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:07.210
Those are folks who are
moving in this world,

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:10.270
also thinking about
anthropologists that engage that...

00:20:10.270 --> 00:20:12.440
I actually think of your work, Christen,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.360
as really moving in that mode of abolition

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:15.193
in the sense that,

00:20:15.193 --> 00:20:16.840
yes, you attend to
anti-Black state violence

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:18.020
in Brazil and the U.S.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.260
But also to the sequela,

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.907
the trail behind that
spectacular event of violence

00:20:22.907 --> 00:20:24.310
and the lives of mothers,

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:26.377
partners and children of the slain.

00:20:26.377 --> 00:20:29.120
And so by tracing those
after lives of violence

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.930
that mirror this threat
and neglect and harm,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:33.300
you make sure that we interrupt,

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.110
yes, this spectacular event,

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:38.550
but also the threads that come
before and after it, right?

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:40.400
You might think that there's
another mode of abolition

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:42.320
that's abolition as a revolution.

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:44.220
Direct confrontation with the state,

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:47.300
a tradition of abolition
that draws its power

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:49.890
directly from ongoing
revolutionary struggle

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.880
led by incarcerated people
and political prisoners.

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.530
So folks like, Joao Costa
Vargas or (indistinct) Britain,

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:59.323
Engage in this tradition,
even within anthropology.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:02.190
And they're not doing
that work as anthropology,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.994
they're doing that work
as anthropologists.

00:21:04.994 --> 00:21:07.370
And so, and trying to
figure out how that happens.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:11.570
You also might look at folks
who use abolition as healing.

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:14.710
Really doing that work of
accountability and transformation

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:17.950
and doing the work of
creating practical safety

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:18.810
in our communities

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:21.490
and transforming the impact
of interpersonal harm.

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:24.200
And so someone like Brendane Tynes,

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:26.090
who works on survivor organizing,

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.890
I think really live us up
that tradition of abolition

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:30.023
in our discipline.

00:21:30.980 --> 00:21:34.170
The last two were thinking
about abolition as geography.

00:21:34.170 --> 00:21:36.650
Building spaces of self-reliance.

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:39.150
And so, I'm looking to
someone like Ashante Reese

00:21:39.150 --> 00:21:41.310
and her work around self-reliance

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:44.110
to help us sketch out what
might that practice look like

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:47.740
in the discipline.

00:21:47.740 --> 00:21:50.250
And finally abolition as culture.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.720
As the practice of finding one another

00:21:52.720 --> 00:21:56.130
and building spaces between people,

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:58.830
because abolition is
something that only lives

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:00.530
in the space between people.

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:03.590
And so here, I'm thinking
about, Deb, your work,

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:07.380
not just the end product of the films,

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:10.530
of the film and the...

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:11.920
So thinking about "Bad Friday,"

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:13.310
thinking about "Bearing Witness,"

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:16.070
but also thinking about
the long form process

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:20.270
to produce those as
operating in that register.

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:22.120
Also like the podcast "Zora's Daughters,"

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:23.660
the Cite Black Women Collective.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:28.390
This work of producing
abolition between us.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:30.340
And then for me, I'll just take it home

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:36.380
with the folks that I learn
most about abolition from

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:38.760
is the Black Organizing Project,

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:41.910
which is an organization
that I'm a member of.

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:44.620
I'm an anthropologist who is
a member of the organization.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:47.370
I'm not an anthropologist
of the organization.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.970
I push in chairs, I tuck in...

00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:51.740
I'm pushing chairs, I make phone calls,

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:53.300
I'm on the phone tree.

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:57.900
This image that you see
here is from a retreat

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:00.290
that some of the organizers put together.

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:01.900
This is the crew of folks

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:05.440
who have been holding the
George Floyd Resolution

00:23:05.440 --> 00:23:08.010
for the elimination of
school police in Oakland,

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.483
which successfully passed in June, 2020.

00:23:11.379 --> 00:23:13.330
The Oakland School Police
Department has been abolished.

00:23:13.330 --> 00:23:16.670
The guns have been melted
down, the cars decommissioned.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.270
They're off the payroll.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:19.820
And in the past year and a half,

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:20.900
it's been this group of people

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:24.450
that have been helping to
create new job descriptions

00:23:24.450 --> 00:23:25.990
for the security staff,

00:23:25.990 --> 00:23:27.560
they have been creating new safety trees

00:23:27.560 --> 00:23:28.620
for different kinds of incidents

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:30.740
at the elementary, middle
school and high school level.

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:34.080
Really doing this dreary week to week work

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.410
of producing abolition in real life.

00:23:36.410 --> 00:23:40.373
And so, I brought them here
as my own guides in this work.

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:43.730
- Thank you so much, Savannah,

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:45.593
so much to think with.

00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:48.610
I'm just thinking about the five...

00:23:48.610 --> 00:23:50.970
I don't know if you
would call them tenants

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:55.750
or entry points or interventions

00:23:55.750 --> 00:23:57.070
in the way that you think of abolition,

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.490
but each one of them gives
us so much to think about.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:01.610
And what I love about that

00:24:01.610 --> 00:24:05.660
is it doesn't begin and
end with an ultimate...

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.820
Rupture is not the
beginning and end, right?

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:10.290
Rupture is one node.

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.230
And so that, there's a way that we can see

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:13.300
the work that we're doing,

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:16.960
the steps towards a bigger rupture.

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:18.530
If we wanna call the
rupture, the revolution,

00:24:18.530 --> 00:24:19.810
there's steps towards that.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:23.380
And there's ways that we
can see that in our work

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:24.810
in the daily work that happens,

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.840
whether it's inside the
academy or outside of it,

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:29.970
so that we don't have to
constantly be operating

00:24:29.970 --> 00:24:33.310
in these false binaries of
activists, non activists

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:35.815
in old school ways that
don't speak to the ways

00:24:35.815 --> 00:24:40.815
that we can be attuned to the
possibility for transformation

00:24:43.670 --> 00:24:45.730
and what might seem
like the smallest acts,

00:24:45.730 --> 00:24:48.480
while still not relying
on those small acts

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.177
to do all of the work.

00:24:50.177 --> 00:24:52.650
But there's a progression in
the way that we understand

00:24:52.650 --> 00:24:55.610
what abolition means
or what it could mean.

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:57.223
So thank you so much for that.

00:24:58.067 --> 00:25:01.183
Christen, I'm gonna turn it over to you.

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:04.723
- Thank you so much.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.530
Before I get started,

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.520
I just wanna make sure I thank everybody.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:11.350
So, I definitely wanna thank you, Aimee,

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:14.010
for pulling this all together.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:17.420
It's beautiful to be in conversation.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:19.690
And I know that we're not in person,

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:21.340
but there's something comforting

00:25:21.340 --> 00:25:26.130
about seeing all of these
wonderful and amazing faces

00:25:26.130 --> 00:25:27.910
that have inspired my work.

00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:30.763
I'm gonna echo Savannah's words,

00:25:31.955 --> 00:25:35.250
Savannah's work, your
work, Aimee, Deb's work

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:37.760
have really inspired the
work that I do as well.

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:40.450
And so I just feel like
I'm in community right now.

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:42.290
And I just wanna say, thank you.

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:45.460
And I also wanna say thank
you to Vanessa Epps as well

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:49.460
for helping us get all
organized prior to this moment.

00:25:49.460 --> 00:25:54.460
And thank you to the Department
of African American Studies

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.340
and the Yale Endeavor Series

00:25:56.340 --> 00:25:59.230
for allowing us to be in conversation.

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:02.990
So I wanna start just really briefly

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:07.460
because I'm not sure how much people know,

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:08.890
who I am or what my work is.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.758
And so I just wanna start by saying,

00:26:11.758 --> 00:26:13.980
I'm gonna talk about

00:26:16.010 --> 00:26:19.770
the anthropology of
liberation and abolition.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:21.040
And I definitely wanna get back

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.890
to some of the things Savannah said,

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:24.900
because Savannah, your interventions

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:27.380
about those five points,
I really appreciate them.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:28.517
And they got me thinking
about some things.

00:26:28.517 --> 00:26:30.667
So, I can't wait to
talk to you about them,

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:36.210
but my work has focused on thinking

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:40.880
about the terrorizing impact
of policing on Black people,

00:26:40.880 --> 00:26:42.570
particularly in Brazil,

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:46.420
but across the Americas
for the past 20 years.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:51.200
And so, when Savannah
mentioned the fact that

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:53.580
in summer of 2020,

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:57.630
when all of a sudden abolition
becomes this mainstream word,

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.340
for many of us who have
been doing this kind of work

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:05.240
around anti-Black policing
for quite some time,

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:06.863
that was a vertigo moment.

00:27:07.710 --> 00:27:10.660
It felt really interesting

00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:14.930
because it meant that the
hegemony was shifting.

00:27:14.930 --> 00:27:17.460
It meant that the terms

00:27:17.460 --> 00:27:20.580
that we have been using
for quite some time

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:22.910
had been pulled out from under us.

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:24.973
And so, I wanna just underscore that

00:27:24.973 --> 00:27:27.150
because I think that that
was part of the thread

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:28.450
that Savannah was getting to.

00:27:28.450 --> 00:27:29.850
But I think that,

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:32.520
one of the provocation
that's in this panel

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.000
is what do we do with the
terms that comfort us?

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:40.060
What do we do with the politics

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.300
that we have become familiar with

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:43.630
and where is it leading us,

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.080
and where do we go with that?

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:48.050
And so,

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:50.300
quickly, my work has been
on the gender dimensions

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:53.140
of anti-Black policing and
particularly the unique frequency

00:27:53.140 --> 00:27:55.343
of Black women's
experiences with policing,

00:27:56.510 --> 00:27:59.820
and what this frequency says
about space, about time,

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.650
about gender and anti-Black violence.

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:04.540
For me, my work,

00:28:04.540 --> 00:28:07.920
and this is gonna sound different
to those of my colleagues

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.670
who are bonafide archeologists.

00:28:10.670 --> 00:28:13.780
But I really think of my
work as archeological,

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:18.690
in that it entails excavating
the cemeteries of Black life

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:20.790
across the Americas,

00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:25.380
through time and space in
order to map, mark and catalog

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:29.150
the artifacts of Black life
in the wake of anti-Blackness.

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:30.790
And I use the phrase in the wake

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:33.400
to follow Christina Sharpe's
brilliant theorizations

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:34.233
of the wake.

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:37.880
So for me, this archeological work

00:28:37.880 --> 00:28:41.130
of tending to the dead and the living is

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:43.337
what Anthropologist
Faye Harrison has called

00:28:43.337 --> 00:28:45.820
"decolonizing anthropology,"

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:48.587
or what Anthropologist
Keisha-Khan Perry has called

00:28:48.587 --> 00:28:50.747
"an anthropology for liberation."

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:52.820
What is that?

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:56.360
Well, to paraphrase their
understandings of it,

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:57.880
it's an anthropological practice

00:28:57.880 --> 00:29:01.850
that focuses on the possibility
of undoing the (stutters),

00:29:01.850 --> 00:29:04.510
excuse me, the coloniality of anthropology

00:29:04.510 --> 00:29:06.220
through the distortion.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.470
And I use that word very specifically

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:10.737
through the distortion
of his normative practice

00:29:10.737 --> 00:29:12.210
and the service of those

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:14.580
that has historically sought to catalog

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:15.933
in order to be colonized.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.823
Or to follow Zora Neale Hurston,

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:24.320
seeking to upturn anthropologies legacy

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:27.760
as the deep practice of white
supremacies need to know.

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:32.680
And so, part of what I see embedded

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:36.630
in the title of this conversation today

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:40.160
is in actuality, a question, right?

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:43.070
Can we have an anthropology
for liberation?

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:46.130
Can we have an anthropology for abolition?

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.890
And what are the internal contradictions

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:50.513
that are inherent therein?

00:29:53.572 --> 00:29:57.870
So for me, intending to
the dead and the living,

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:02.870
and I wanna underscore that my
work intending to the living

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:07.930
has necessarily been in
community with the dead,

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:09.600
because most of the times

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.180
when I'm doing my ethnographic
and anthropological work,

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:16.590
I'm working with those
who have been left behind

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:20.220
after their loved ones have
been killed by the police.

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:23.873
Which is a very grief based,

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:29.830
and tender practice.

00:30:29.830 --> 00:30:31.730
I'll just say it that way.

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:35.900
And so one of the things that
I am constantly attentive to,

00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:38.430
and this is something that
I also find inspiration

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:40.360
in Deb's work.

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:43.310
Particularly around
witnessing and thinking about

00:30:43.310 --> 00:30:45.850
how do we have these conversations

00:30:45.850 --> 00:30:48.710
in the aftermath of violence,

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:52.580
but how do we excavate our
stories without violating,

00:30:52.580 --> 00:30:54.393
yet again, our graves?

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:56.760
After all,

00:30:56.760 --> 00:31:00.360
grave robbing is a traditional
fundamental element

00:31:00.360 --> 00:31:04.383
of the anthropological/archeological
process of excavation.

00:31:05.710 --> 00:31:06.910
In conducting my research,

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:10.050
I constantly grapple with
this fraught tension,

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:12.160
rigorous qualitative research,

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.690
at least within the bounds
of traditional anthropology

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:17.640
seems to require that we as ethnographers

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:19.580
journey into the communities of the dead

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:23.110
to peel back the layers of
people's lives and sentiments

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:26.363
in order to discover the truths
of the past and the present.

00:31:27.250 --> 00:31:30.300
Yet there is something
deeply unsettling to me

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:32.720
about this process that
pushes us to rethink

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:35.010
the meaning of liberation.

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:37.200
What does Black liberation look like

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:39.623
and what does it entail?

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:47.040
So, as somebody who spent
my entire career doing work

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:50.700
in collaboration with
Black movements in Brazil,

00:31:50.700 --> 00:31:55.550
I have learned a lot from
them and from those movements.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:32:00.220
And so I wanna situate
what I'm about to present

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:04.650
as learning from, as
opposed to studying on,

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.940
because I think that that's
also one of the themes

00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:08.730
that comes through in this conversation.

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:10.610
What does it mean for us to engage

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:12.670
in collaborative processes

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:15.760
that are not based on extraction

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:18.920
or somehow based on
the continued processes

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:21.873
or the continued practice of genocide,

00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:29.560
for lack of a better word?

00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:33.320
But instead seek to learn from,

00:32:33.320 --> 00:32:38.320
and be in community with folk
in order to find solutions

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:43.420
to our common experiences,

00:32:45.300 --> 00:32:49.770
as people with targets on our backs,

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:50.820
I'll put it that way.

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:55.680
And so one of the things
that I've learned is that

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:59.220
if we are to possibly have an
anthropology for liberation,

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:00.210
whatever that looks like,

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:03.830
and I love the questions that
you put to the front, Aimee,

00:33:03.830 --> 00:33:06.830
because I'm not convinced of anthropology.

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:08.380
I'm also not convinced

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:10.790
that anthropology and liberation
can be in conversation

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:11.730
with one another.

00:33:11.730 --> 00:33:15.320
That is definitely not
something that I want to reify.

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:17.500
What I do want to do is to say,

00:33:17.500 --> 00:33:19.130
if that is a possibility,

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:22.233
if there is something there,
what might it look like?

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:28.860
And for me to center Black
life and Black freedom

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:31.383
must necessarily upturn imperialism,

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:36.380
upturn heterosexism, upturn misogyny,

00:33:36.380 --> 00:33:38.440
in addition to anti-Blackness.

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:40.530
And I put those things alongside that,

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:44.480
because I think despite our efforts,

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:49.480
we still engage in the
incessant practice of linearity

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:54.310
when it comes to understanding oppression,

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:57.290
meaning when we're talking
about anti-Blackness,

00:33:57.290 --> 00:34:01.300
we tend to think about it
as separate from gender,

00:34:01.300 --> 00:34:03.793
as separate from imperialism,

00:34:04.700 --> 00:34:09.660
as separate from various
heterosexism and things like that.

00:34:09.660 --> 00:34:12.790
And I think that that is still a frontier,

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:15.363
as familiar as that may sound.

00:34:16.408 --> 00:34:19.620
And so, in thinking about that,

00:34:19.620 --> 00:34:23.260
I have started to think about
the question or the practice,

00:34:23.260 --> 00:34:25.569
the historical practice of Quilombo,

00:34:25.569 --> 00:34:27.501
or (indistinct).

00:34:27.501 --> 00:34:30.740
Which is the practice
in marronage in Brazil.

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:35.080
And Savannah, when you made
your comments about abolition

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:39.950
and abolition as territory making

00:34:40.967 --> 00:34:42.910
and community building, et cetera,

00:34:42.910 --> 00:34:45.120
I couldn't help but think
about the practice of quilombo

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:46.710
in conversation with that,

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:51.710
because despite the fact
that the word abolition

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:54.620
does not come up in the
conversations in Brazil

00:34:54.620 --> 00:34:57.090
around quilombo, right?

00:34:57.090 --> 00:34:59.800
We can think about these
things in conversation,

00:34:59.800 --> 00:35:01.633
in diasporic conversation.

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:05.580
And specifically thinking
about the practice

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:07.800
of quilombo to follow,
(speaks foreign language),

00:35:08.800 --> 00:35:13.800
which is this idea, not only
of building spaces of refuge,

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:16.043
but of building war encampment,

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:19.560
and the reason why I want
to bring out the idea

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:21.400
of war encampments

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:23.840
is because I think that

00:35:23.840 --> 00:35:26.680
one of the things that may allow us

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:29.580
to think about an
anthropology for liberation

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:33.640
is to think about it as offensive,

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:35.869
as an offensive tactic,

00:35:35.869 --> 00:35:39.400
not as a defensive tactic

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:42.170
and embedded within the notion of quilombo

00:35:42.170 --> 00:35:46.430
is an inherent understanding
of the ecologies of violence

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:48.220
that we are up against.

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:49.810
It is an inherent understanding

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:52.740
that we must build spaces of refuge.

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:57.310
We must build spaces that
will proliferate Black life

00:35:57.310 --> 00:35:59.370
and reproduce Black life,

00:35:59.370 --> 00:36:02.770
but entailed in that
is defending ourselves

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:04.253
in some way, shape or form.

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:07.710
Whether that mean taking the high ground

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:10.090
and establishing ourselves
in the mountains,

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:13.110
or that mean setting up
autonomous economies,

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:16.190
or that mean somehow figuring out a way

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:18.820
to fight back against our oppressors.

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:19.703
And so,

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:22.900
I want to put that on the table,

00:36:22.900 --> 00:36:24.680
because I think that

00:36:24.680 --> 00:36:27.010
in order for us to think about

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:30.730
the possibilities of
anthropology and liberation,

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:33.250
there must be some sort of break

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:35.320
going back to something
that also Savanna said,

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:36.730
there must be some sort of break

00:36:36.730 --> 00:36:38.423
with anthropology as we know it.

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:41.703
And that break,

00:36:45.782 --> 00:36:48.640
has got to be defined clearly.

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:51.600
And so if we know anything about marronage

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:53.306
and we know anything about quilombo,

00:36:53.306 --> 00:36:55.200
the practice of quilombo.

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:59.170
In many ways, these are
territories that are created

00:36:59.170 --> 00:37:01.000
and autonomous spaces that are created

00:37:01.000 --> 00:37:03.463
by people who escaped slavery.

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:08.550
Enslaved Africans who fled
the institution of slavery

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:10.520
to create autonomous societies,

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:12.590
and then fortify themselves

00:37:12.590 --> 00:37:15.140
against the onslaught of violence

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:18.200
that was in opposition to Black freedom.

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:20.800
What we know is that quilombos

00:37:20.800 --> 00:37:23.353
are never a outside of the colonial space.

00:37:24.360 --> 00:37:28.370
Quilombos are never located in an area

00:37:28.370 --> 00:37:32.520
that is beyond the zone of oppression.

00:37:32.520 --> 00:37:34.540
And so I think for me,

00:37:34.540 --> 00:37:36.690
that's a helpful place for us to think

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:40.090
about what an anthropology of
liberation might look like.

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:43.590
Because to think that
we are gonna get out of,

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.640
the colonial zone of
oppression of anthropology

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:50.270
by practicing anthropology,
is not realistic,

00:37:50.270 --> 00:37:52.570
but what does it mean to set up

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:55.420
fortified enclaves of resistance

00:37:55.420 --> 00:37:58.480
within a colonial space of war

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:01.320
that will fortify us

00:38:01.320 --> 00:38:03.653
and lead to the possibility
of our liberation.

00:38:05.040 --> 00:38:08.007
And so, to me, this
notion of (indistinct),

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.250
is one that we might be able to use

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:14.970
if we are to embrace the possibility

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:16.570
of putting these three terms

00:38:16.570 --> 00:38:18.460
into conversation with one another

00:38:18.460 --> 00:38:20.927
and I'll end it there, thank you.

00:38:20.927 --> 00:38:22.100
- Ah, thank you so much,

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:22.950
Christen, there's so much...

00:38:22.950 --> 00:38:25.970
I wanna move to Deb but I just
wanna name really quickly,

00:38:25.970 --> 00:38:27.300
there's so much in what you said,

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:30.620
but I kept coming back to
something you said early on

00:38:30.620 --> 00:38:33.010
about the terms that comfort us.

00:38:33.010 --> 00:38:34.740
And then I started to think about the way

00:38:34.740 --> 00:38:38.470
that terminology and
concepts without action

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:41.140
become sort of inebriate us.

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:44.100
The seduction and
inebriation of these terms

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:46.140
that don't have an action tied to it.

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:48.480
But then I was also
thinking about the ways

00:38:48.480 --> 00:38:51.240
that language is a trap and confix.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:52.500
And in some ways,

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:56.370
not totally capture what
is happening on the ground.

00:38:56.370 --> 00:38:57.620
So I'm just gonna briefly...

00:38:57.620 --> 00:38:59.620
And some of you have
heard this story about

00:38:59.620 --> 00:39:02.440
how I came to anthropology
as an undergrad.

00:39:02.440 --> 00:39:03.490
I was taking a course

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:06.340
that I did not know was
an anthropology course.

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:08.840
So it was an upper level
anthropology course

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:11.620
and we were reading these
beautiful ethnographies.

00:39:11.620 --> 00:39:12.870
And to me,

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:15.780
what I recognized in what
was called anthropology,

00:39:15.780 --> 00:39:17.820
but I didn't know it was called that yet,

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:19.360
until like the middle of the semester.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:21.030
I realized what we were up to.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:24.130
It was very much to me, a
Black feminist practice,

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:25.560
because the way that we were talking

00:39:25.560 --> 00:39:29.850
about these ethnographies and
the practice of field work

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:33.040
was very much about how
you come into a community,

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:34.920
how you learn, how to be in community,

00:39:34.920 --> 00:39:37.410
how you immerse and humble yourself,

00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:40.890
what it means to take people's
experiences seriously,

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:44.690
to listen to stories and to be a witness

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:46.170
more than a documentarian.

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:47.830
And this is the way that I,

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:50.400
without the name of anthropology,

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:52.640
there was a way, so if we think about,

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:55.510
what is the there, there,
what could be retrieved?

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:58.390
I'm wondering if the terminology keeps us

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:00.600
from seeing what can be retrieved.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:04.540
The labeling of the thing
keeps us from seeing

00:40:04.540 --> 00:40:07.960
what is possible to, in
your terminology, Christen,

00:40:07.960 --> 00:40:10.840
excavate from practices
that we're already doing

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:13.610
without having to fix it to a name.

00:40:13.610 --> 00:40:16.630
So I just wanted to speak to some of that,

00:40:16.630 --> 00:40:18.010
there's so much more in what you said,

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:19.480
but I'll just leave it there

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:21.673
because I wanna pass it on to Deb.

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:29.160
- Well, thank you very much, Aimee.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.510
And I echo all of the thank yous

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:35.410
that Christen and Savannah
have already put forward.

00:40:35.410 --> 00:40:37.480
Thank you for gathering us.

00:40:37.480 --> 00:40:40.233
Thank you, Vanessa, for organizing us.

00:40:41.606 --> 00:40:44.520
And I love being in dialogue with y'all

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:48.453
and have missed the sort of in person,

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:54.360
moments of huddle and hugs

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.440
and laughter,

00:40:56.440 --> 00:40:58.880
and really good food

00:40:58.880 --> 00:41:02.970
and conspiring together.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:04.913
And I think that is something that,

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:09.390
we've really lost over the last two years.

00:41:09.390 --> 00:41:13.953
And I think that's also
a big sense of strength,

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:18.330
for many of us going back
to our own institution.

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:20.410
So these moments when we get to

00:41:24.653 --> 00:41:26.980
draw energy from that care

00:41:26.980 --> 00:41:28.560
that we experience with each other.

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:29.940
So we do it online.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:31.020
It's not quite the same,

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:35.003
but it is lovely to see your faces.

00:41:37.040 --> 00:41:42.040
Aimee, where you sort of just intervened,

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:45.173
I think is where I might start,

00:41:46.830 --> 00:41:49.710
which has to do with the
question of anthropology

00:41:49.710 --> 00:41:52.190
and the extent to which
we situate ourselves

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:55.210
within a few called anthropology.

00:41:55.210 --> 00:41:57.870
And you and I have had
this conversation before,

00:41:57.870 --> 00:42:01.610
and I'm sure it's true of many of us here

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:03.603
in this space and beyond,

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:09.920
but I think like many folk I
sort of fell into anthropology.

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:16.003
I did not apply to PhD
programs in anthropology.

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:20.940
I had been dancing as Aimee
said, with Urban Bush Women.

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:25.160
And even before that,

00:42:25.160 --> 00:42:27.480
had always been working through theater

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:30.820
or other kind of performative practices

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:33.850
as a way to enter community,

00:42:33.850 --> 00:42:38.850
as a way to think together about
becoming conscious together

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:43.990
of the problems or issues within community

00:42:43.990 --> 00:42:46.130
that we wanted to confront.

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:49.500
And then finding ways
through creative practices,

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:54.500
through the arts, through just
embodied pedagogy, I suppose,

00:42:57.170 --> 00:42:59.280
to attend to some of those issues.

00:42:59.280 --> 00:43:02.630
And that was my background as a performer.

00:43:02.630 --> 00:43:05.400
And that's what sort of
drew me into the academy,

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:09.707
but not directly into anthropology.

00:43:09.707 --> 00:43:12.130
And (indistinct) I had applied to

00:43:12.130 --> 00:43:14.260
Latin American and
Caribbean studies program

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:15.570
and that's where I started.

00:43:15.570 --> 00:43:19.600
And one of my first classes
was an anthropology class.

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:24.600
And I just remember recognizing,

00:43:24.700 --> 00:43:26.120
again, like you, Aimee,

00:43:26.120 --> 00:43:29.860
in the method of what we were reading,

00:43:29.860 --> 00:43:31.140
that that was something

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:33.170
that I felt like I was already doing.

00:43:33.170 --> 00:43:36.283
And that I didn't realize
that there was an name for.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:38.870
And then when I took...

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:41.350
And that class was called
transnational processes.

00:43:41.350 --> 00:43:42.930
So you can imagine,

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:45.940
with the person who would become
my advisor, Connie Sutton.

00:43:45.940 --> 00:43:47.880
You can imagine it was a seminar,

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:51.090
it was a progressive take on globalization

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:53.610
and historicizing the inequalities

00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:56.620
that were becoming exacerbated,

00:43:56.620 --> 00:43:59.763
in late 80s through the 90s.

00:44:01.100 --> 00:44:03.910
And when I then did the backtracking

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:05.830
to do the history of anthropology

00:44:05.830 --> 00:44:08.710
and the theory of anthropology
is when I understood

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:10.463
what I was getting myself into.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:15.680
And when I think, again, for many of us,

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:19.860
you consciously suspend disbelief

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:22.040
in order to get to the
thing that you think,

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:23.973
you know you're already doing.

00:44:26.131 --> 00:44:27.670
So,

00:44:27.670 --> 00:44:29.963
yes, I'm an anthropologist.

00:44:30.955 --> 00:44:34.130
I love it for the things
that you're talking about

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:38.490
for the method of learning,

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:41.220
for the method of being in community,

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:46.180
for a mode of gathering

00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:50.563
that can enact something in
the world that needs to happen.

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:55.400
But ultimately, I guess
the way I consider myself

00:44:55.400 --> 00:45:00.400
is as someone who assembles archives,

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:04.270
archives, in my case, of violence

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:06.890
and of state violence in particular

00:45:06.890 --> 00:45:09.060
and of struggles against state violence

00:45:09.060 --> 00:45:10.700
and visions of a different world

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:12.170
in the face of state violence.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:15.170
But these are archives
that could be stories.

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:17.060
They could be performative,

00:45:17.060 --> 00:45:20.750
they could be sonic, they could be visual.

00:45:20.750 --> 00:45:24.870
And the archive itself is not the end.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:27.570
The archive is then a tool

00:45:28.570 --> 00:45:30.600
in order to create something else.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:35.403
And so I think that's the
basis of the scholarly work

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:40.070
that I do that ends up
in articles and in books,

00:45:40.070 --> 00:45:42.310
but also the scholarly work that I do

00:45:42.310 --> 00:45:47.050
that ends up in community
festivals or films

00:45:47.050 --> 00:45:48.790
or performances,

00:45:48.790 --> 00:45:52.170
or other kinds of institution building.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:55.333
And not everything is for everybody.

00:45:57.040 --> 00:46:02.040
Not every iteration of an
idea is for all audiences.

00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:03.020
And so to me,

00:46:03.020 --> 00:46:06.600
it's so important to think about audience

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:09.050
when thinking about the
work that we're doing,

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:11.810
what do I want this to do in the world?

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:13.450
And therefore, who is it for

00:46:13.450 --> 00:46:15.580
and what form must it take

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:18.270
in order to do what it's supposed to do

00:46:18.270 --> 00:46:19.853
for the people it is for?

00:46:22.220 --> 00:46:27.220
So that takes on many
different kinds of things.

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:29.680
And I guess what...

00:46:30.640 --> 00:46:31.900
It could have been anthropology,

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:33.320
it could have been many other things.

00:46:33.320 --> 00:46:36.290
And I didn't go into this
to become a professor

00:46:36.290 --> 00:46:38.423
that happened at the very last minute,

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:41.060
but there could have been

00:46:41.060 --> 00:46:44.010
many other professional paths as well.

00:46:44.010 --> 00:46:49.010
I do feel lucky to be in a
space where I have the time,

00:46:51.430 --> 00:46:53.070
although it never feels like I have time,

00:46:53.070 --> 00:46:56.320
but in theory I have the time

00:46:56.320 --> 00:46:59.973
and absolutely have the
resources to distribute,

00:47:00.885 --> 00:47:01.718
in other ways.

00:47:01.718 --> 00:47:06.718
And I guess I see that
also as part of a mission,

00:47:07.180 --> 00:47:12.180
which is to use the resources
of this very wealthy,

00:47:14.740 --> 00:47:18.210
very large, very decentralized,

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:20.550
in many ways, amazing university

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:25.550
toward the furthering
of community projects

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:29.240
within Philadelphia and beyond.

00:47:29.240 --> 00:47:30.680
So again, that looks like many things,

00:47:30.680 --> 00:47:33.190
it looks like a Kumbla Festival

00:47:33.190 --> 00:47:36.470
that a friend and I organize
every year in Jamaica,

00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:40.710
or it looks like the conversation
that we're hoping to host

00:47:40.710 --> 00:47:43.920
during the Kumbla Festival
about land dispossession,

00:47:43.920 --> 00:47:46.650
in the particular community,

00:47:46.650 --> 00:47:49.360
but also tying that to what's
going on and nationally

00:47:49.360 --> 00:47:51.450
and transnationally,

00:47:51.450 --> 00:47:55.100
or it looks like this article
that I'm really late on

00:47:55.100 --> 00:47:58.070
about sovereignty that
I'm trying to put together

00:47:58.070 --> 00:47:59.210
for a volume,

00:47:59.210 --> 00:48:03.150
or it's a new film that we're working on

00:48:03.150 --> 00:48:06.840
that really dissects the 70s in Jamaica

00:48:06.840 --> 00:48:09.490
by looking at the Ethiopian
Zion Coptic Church

00:48:09.490 --> 00:48:14.453
and the bizarre
intervention they made into,

00:48:15.930 --> 00:48:17.213
socialist,

00:48:18.370 --> 00:48:19.730
politics

00:48:19.730 --> 00:48:22.330
and Rastafari,

00:48:22.330 --> 00:48:25.303
race relations, I guess
I could put it that way.

00:48:27.320 --> 00:48:28.710
And I think,

00:48:28.710 --> 00:48:32.060
though I am situated in
an anthropology department

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:35.640
and have actually been editor
of an anthropology journal.

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:39.790
I am often not considered
an anthropologist

00:48:39.790 --> 00:48:44.400
or I have heard or overheard,

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:47.030
I guess, people looking at my book,

00:48:47.030 --> 00:48:49.417
my most recent book or
other work and saying,

00:48:49.417 --> 00:48:52.581
"Oh, it's great, but is it anthropology?"

00:48:52.581 --> 00:48:53.713
So I think that,

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:58.100
and I'm getting to the
abolition question in a minute,

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:00.133
but I think that,

00:49:02.290 --> 00:49:07.290
while those of us who are
intentionally attempting

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:14.483
to transform the disciplinary
space that we're in,

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:20.063
are working through the
language of abolition

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:24.130
or of decolonizing

00:49:24.130 --> 00:49:27.840
or liberation,

00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:30.870
some of the other words
that have been used today.

00:49:30.870 --> 00:49:33.860
I think it's also important
not to be too attached

00:49:33.860 --> 00:49:35.280
to the label.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:39.920
I think we would be fighting
those battles anywhere

00:49:39.920 --> 00:49:43.743
and in any of the spaces that we occupy.

00:49:45.330 --> 00:49:49.040
So, some of the work that
I think is super important

00:49:49.040 --> 00:49:52.980
or that I spend a lot
of my time on right now

00:49:52.980 --> 00:49:56.510
is also creating spaces
within this institution

00:49:57.355 --> 00:50:02.330
that ultimately are about
undoing the institution.

00:50:02.330 --> 00:50:05.470
And our center is a space like that.

00:50:05.470 --> 00:50:07.400
The Center for Experimental Ethnography.

00:50:07.400 --> 00:50:09.830
And we were very privileged to host Aimee

00:50:09.830 --> 00:50:13.140
as one of our first fellows,

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:18.140
but that center is organized
to support faculty and students

00:50:18.170 --> 00:50:19.920
who have a creative practice

00:50:19.920 --> 00:50:22.500
as the basis of their research process.

00:50:22.500 --> 00:50:24.200
And we have had filmmakers,

00:50:24.200 --> 00:50:26.350
we have had dancers and choreographers,

00:50:26.350 --> 00:50:28.620
we've had visual artists, textile artists,

00:50:28.620 --> 00:50:32.563
we've had sound artists,

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:34.510
creative writers.

00:50:34.510 --> 00:50:36.310
This semester we had Amitav Gosh

00:50:36.310 --> 00:50:39.440
and we staged a musical
version of a parable

00:50:39.440 --> 00:50:40.763
that he recently wrote.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:47.890
And the point is to understand
artists as intellectuals

00:50:49.830 --> 00:50:54.030
and creative work as intellectual work

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:56.570
and to therefore transform

00:50:56.570 --> 00:50:58.800
the modes of knowledge production.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:02.250
So in a way, it's trying
to bring the university

00:51:02.250 --> 00:51:04.570
to what I understood
as knowledge production

00:51:04.570 --> 00:51:08.450
before entering the university.

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:13.450
So in that way, it is about decolonizing.

00:51:13.780 --> 00:51:17.170
And in that respect, I also would say that

00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:19.333
all of these words that we use,

00:51:21.545 --> 00:51:24.100
I tend to use them in the gerund form.

00:51:25.210 --> 00:51:27.730
Even to think if we agree

00:51:27.730 --> 00:51:31.590
that sovereignty is a
process and a practice,

00:51:31.590 --> 00:51:35.380
and that liberation is a
process and a practice,

00:51:35.380 --> 00:51:38.280
then obviously decolonizing and abolishing

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:40.810
are also processes and practices

00:51:40.810 --> 00:51:42.050
and they're not events,

00:51:42.050 --> 00:51:44.150
they're not something that
happens and we can say,

00:51:44.150 --> 00:51:45.700
Oh, we're done.

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:48.100
We've solved that problem,

00:51:48.100 --> 00:51:52.300
it's an ongoing modality of life.

00:51:52.300 --> 00:51:55.210
And I think that...

00:51:55.210 --> 00:51:58.163
Oh, thank you for
putting that in the chat.

00:52:00.530 --> 00:52:03.553
That guides me,

00:52:05.153 --> 00:52:09.750
in everything I do.

00:52:09.750 --> 00:52:11.890
So like in considering,

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:12.950
am I gonna give this talk

00:52:12.950 --> 00:52:15.030
or am I gonna work on this project

00:52:15.030 --> 00:52:16.900
or are we gonna co-sponsor this event?

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:18.340
Or whatever it is,

00:52:18.340 --> 00:52:20.100
it's who is it serving?

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:21.240
Who is the audience?

00:52:21.240 --> 00:52:24.250
How does it move through a process

00:52:24.250 --> 00:52:29.250
and practice of decolonizing,
abolishing, liberating?

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:34.310
And how does it create joy in
our worlds and in our lives?

00:52:36.890 --> 00:52:37.830
- Thank you so much.

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:41.520
I, as you were speaking, Deb,

00:52:41.520 --> 00:52:44.810
was thinking about how much
of the work of abolition

00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:46.313
is about remembering.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:49.690
What I knew before I came to this,

00:52:49.690 --> 00:52:51.170
what I was already doing,

00:52:51.170 --> 00:52:53.340
what I had been doing and that,

00:52:53.340 --> 00:52:55.580
through the training and the disciplining

00:52:55.580 --> 00:52:59.370
of our minds and our bodies and...

00:52:59.370 --> 00:53:01.603
Context, post all of that,

00:53:02.774 --> 00:53:05.860
that maybe the first step in
abolition is a remembering,

00:53:05.860 --> 00:53:07.837
a deep honoring of what we already know,

00:53:07.837 --> 00:53:10.193
and we hold in our bodies
and in our spirits.

00:53:11.110 --> 00:53:13.780
And I just wanna say,
for everybody listening,

00:53:13.780 --> 00:53:15.440
I know you're listening intently,

00:53:15.440 --> 00:53:18.090
there's some questions in
the chat that I wanna get to.

00:53:18.090 --> 00:53:21.860
And what I've tried to do as you have been

00:53:21.860 --> 00:53:23.030
giving your opening remarks

00:53:23.030 --> 00:53:25.980
is just pepper in some
things that I wanna expand on

00:53:25.980 --> 00:53:27.850
in between your remarks

00:53:27.850 --> 00:53:30.230
and really, because what
you're saying is so rich

00:53:30.230 --> 00:53:31.960
and so layered,

00:53:31.960 --> 00:53:34.060
you've kind of hit on the other prompts

00:53:34.060 --> 00:53:35.110
that I wanted to ask.

00:53:35.110 --> 00:53:38.730
So I wanna make space for us
to be in genuine conversation

00:53:38.730 --> 00:53:39.780
because people always say that,

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:42.357
like, "It's just a conversation
and then it's not."

00:53:43.600 --> 00:53:45.957
And I think Christen said this,
was the first one to say it,

00:53:45.957 --> 00:53:49.320
oh, I wanna ask you more
about this, to Savannah.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:50.330
So I wanna open up space

00:53:50.330 --> 00:53:54.000
before we move to the
questions in the chat

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:56.410
for you all to talk to one another,

00:53:56.410 --> 00:53:58.890
if there's questions that
you have of one another

00:53:58.890 --> 00:54:01.560
about your work, about
something that you said today,

00:54:01.560 --> 00:54:05.032
I just wanna allow that space
for us to practice conv...

00:54:05.032 --> 00:54:07.112
(indistinct) to practice conversation

00:54:07.112 --> 00:54:10.823
(laughs) for us to be in conversation.

00:54:12.830 --> 00:54:13.750
So I'll just open it up.

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:15.010
I don't know if there was something

00:54:15.010 --> 00:54:16.586
that you wanted to start with, Christen.

00:54:16.586 --> 00:54:19.080
- I was gonna say, I'm
like jumping into the line.

00:54:19.080 --> 00:54:21.623
- Yeah, please.
- (laughs) A couple questions.

00:54:22.840 --> 00:54:24.020
Exactly, Savannah.

00:54:24.020 --> 00:54:25.020
I'm like jumping in.

00:54:27.950 --> 00:54:30.220
I have two questions
and one is for Savannah

00:54:30.220 --> 00:54:31.483
and one is for Deb.

00:54:32.760 --> 00:54:35.640
I would really appreciate,

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:37.940
because part of what is
really inspiring to me

00:54:37.940 --> 00:54:39.460
about your work, Savannah,

00:54:39.460 --> 00:54:43.250
is just how much practice or praxis

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:48.740
is the center of your
work on abolition, right?

00:54:48.740 --> 00:54:50.320
And so everything that you do

00:54:50.320 --> 00:54:52.700
is in conversation with community

00:54:52.700 --> 00:54:54.440
and in thinking about

00:54:54.440 --> 00:54:57.770
those five understandings of abolition,

00:54:57.770 --> 00:55:00.630
I just wanted to hear you
talk a little bit more

00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:05.960
about how we create those
possibilities in community

00:55:05.960 --> 00:55:07.560
and what does that it look like?

00:55:08.712 --> 00:55:13.712
And for Deb, I am always
inspired by your research

00:55:14.530 --> 00:55:17.810
and all of the amazing
community work that you do,

00:55:17.810 --> 00:55:21.110
but I am also inspired by
the ways that you engage

00:55:21.110 --> 00:55:23.520
in institution building and disruption,

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:26.140
which is one of the
things that you mentioned.

00:55:26.140 --> 00:55:27.360
And one of the things that,

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:30.830
I think has been a key aspect of that

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:38.590
is your ability to bridge
community with the university.

00:55:38.590 --> 00:55:41.060
And so, I wanted to hear
you talk a little bit more

00:55:41.060 --> 00:55:42.340
about how we do that work,

00:55:42.340 --> 00:55:46.330
because I think that that
is part of the process

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:50.500
of decolonizing or
liberating or abolishing,

00:55:50.500 --> 00:55:52.563
whatever gerund we want to use.

00:55:53.610 --> 00:55:58.020
I think at the heart of this
conversation is that process

00:55:58.020 --> 00:56:00.293
of breaking down the university walls.

00:56:01.210 --> 00:56:03.690
And I think that CCE
is a good example of...

00:56:03.690 --> 00:56:06.010
Excuse me, CCE is a good example of that.

00:56:06.010 --> 00:56:08.960
And I wanted to hear you talk
a little bit more about that.

00:56:11.430 --> 00:56:13.730
- I love these questions,
look and before...

00:56:13.730 --> 00:56:15.370
I know it's not my turn
to ask the question,

00:56:15.370 --> 00:56:17.210
but I'm just gonna say it anyway,

00:56:17.210 --> 00:56:20.113
when you said the hegemony shifting.

00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:22.530
That is so, I mean...

00:56:22.530 --> 00:56:23.870
I don't know if my trekkies are out there,

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:25.860
but it's like the Borg is adapting.

00:56:25.860 --> 00:56:29.127
It's just thinking
about the ways in which,

00:56:29.127 --> 00:56:30.577
"Oh, Charlie, see they
got me, they got me.

00:56:30.577 --> 00:56:33.430
"I just said it, I just said
the ways in which they got me."

00:56:33.430 --> 00:56:38.430
Thinking about how
malleable and how adapted,

00:56:38.900 --> 00:56:43.900
how like fundamentally creative
death and captivity are.

00:56:44.150 --> 00:56:46.363
And so I think there's that element.

00:56:47.580 --> 00:56:51.490
And so I think these five
gears, this is just...

00:56:51.490 --> 00:56:54.390
I mean, it's like five stars
in the night sky, right?

00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:57.680
It's not a container.

00:56:57.680 --> 00:57:00.110
They're just kind of points to look at.

00:57:00.110 --> 00:57:02.680
And so I think part of our
job is not to fit into these,

00:57:02.680 --> 00:57:04.900
but just to make more and more,

00:57:04.900 --> 00:57:05.960
clear the clouds out the way

00:57:05.960 --> 00:57:10.083
so we can see all of the ways
our community is getting free.

00:57:11.170 --> 00:57:12.090
So that's one piece.

00:57:12.090 --> 00:57:16.370
So I think, I am oriented
towards a proliferation

00:57:16.370 --> 00:57:17.903
approach to abolition.

00:57:19.068 --> 00:57:21.960
One in which, I think I said
something about this earlier,

00:57:21.960 --> 00:57:23.350
somebody was saying something about,

00:57:23.350 --> 00:57:26.300
oh, going towards abolition.

00:57:26.300 --> 00:57:29.990
And so I think, certainly
in terms of a revolutionary

00:57:29.990 --> 00:57:32.590
tradition of abolition or
one that's oriented around

00:57:34.460 --> 00:57:37.490
the end of the prison industrial complex,

00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:39.890
that's a very clear end goal.

00:57:39.890 --> 00:57:44.880
And the prison industrial
complex is a giant black squid

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:45.733
of a leviathan.

00:57:47.777 --> 00:57:50.540
So it's one thing to close
all the prisons in the world.

00:57:50.540 --> 00:57:54.360
And so, similar I'll think
of it in terms of practice,

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:56.983
the Oakland School Police
Department is abolished.

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:00.730
And the day the last cop left schools,

00:58:00.730 --> 00:58:01.963
are our children safe?

00:58:03.400 --> 00:58:04.233
No.

00:58:05.560 --> 00:58:08.100
One particular kind of vacuum of captivity

00:58:08.100 --> 00:58:09.997
does not actually produce
the world that we need.

00:58:09.997 --> 00:58:11.250
And to me, that's the distinction

00:58:11.250 --> 00:58:13.233
between abolition and liberation.

00:58:15.170 --> 00:58:17.150
I mean, I know Ruth Gilmore,

00:58:17.150 --> 00:58:18.690
has the amazing conception of thinking

00:58:18.690 --> 00:58:19.910
of abolition as growing things.

00:58:19.910 --> 00:58:22.880
Again, these are different
ways of thinking about it.

00:58:22.880 --> 00:58:24.780
They're all gonna work, but for me,

00:58:24.780 --> 00:58:28.610
it's helpful to think about
abolition as that vacuum,

00:58:28.610 --> 00:58:30.380
and that doesn't actually take

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:32.600
any of the responsibility away from us

00:58:32.600 --> 00:58:34.914
to build a world worth living in.

00:58:34.914 --> 00:58:36.420
And that that's not,

00:58:36.420 --> 00:58:38.220
it's not only not an end goal,

00:58:38.220 --> 00:58:40.829
it's also not anywhere near enough.

00:58:40.829 --> 00:58:42.680
And so that's part of what
I'm concerned about too,

00:58:42.680 --> 00:58:45.340
that all of a sudden the
floor has become the ceiling.

00:58:45.340 --> 00:58:48.770
When the end of captivity
has become our dream of...

00:58:51.347 --> 00:58:52.740
That's what we go to the altar for.

00:58:52.740 --> 00:58:54.010
That's what we're pouring libation for

00:58:54.010 --> 00:58:55.807
just to not be in captivity.

00:58:55.807 --> 00:58:57.090
And so I don't think so,

00:58:57.090 --> 00:58:59.870
but I think part of that
cohabitation of language

00:58:59.870 --> 00:59:03.683
can also be a domestication
of political imaginaries.

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:07.610
And so for me, I'm really
invested in abolition

00:59:07.610 --> 00:59:12.120
because I like destroying things.

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:14.950
(laughs) That's my gift.

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:17.083
I like breaking, I like ripping.

00:59:19.068 --> 00:59:22.651
(stutters) We need a team.

00:59:26.150 --> 00:59:27.270
So I guess for me,

00:59:27.270 --> 00:59:30.630
nobody mistake my particular
interest in destruction

00:59:30.630 --> 00:59:34.660
with a political program,
because that is not it.

00:59:34.660 --> 00:59:37.140
It's gonna be way more, that's one thing.

00:59:37.140 --> 00:59:41.400
And so then when you said,
how do we do this work.

00:59:41.400 --> 00:59:42.550
Dr. Smith, who is "we?"

00:59:45.690 --> 00:59:47.413
And I'm asking that because, I think-

00:59:47.413 --> 00:59:49.230
- I was literally thinking about "we"

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:51.520
as in the small we in the room,

00:59:51.520 --> 00:59:54.470
but I really take that point
'cause that's a good one.

00:59:54.470 --> 00:59:57.193
'Cause that's part of what
happened in summer 2020.

00:59:59.050 --> 00:59:59.960
- And it also happens...

00:59:59.960 --> 01:00:01.907
I'll just say, as like boujee Black people

01:00:01.907 --> 01:00:05.218
who have moved to another
place, paying a very high rent.

01:00:05.218 --> 01:00:07.570
Not always, but often the
place that we don't live

01:00:07.570 --> 01:00:11.450
or live in a different
neighborhood than we grew up in.

01:00:11.450 --> 01:00:13.130
And so I think for me it's important

01:00:13.130 --> 01:00:16.870
that who is "we" is not just about like,

01:00:16.870 --> 01:00:18.400
race, class, gender.

01:00:18.400 --> 01:00:22.757
But also about belonging and
different layers of indigeneity

01:00:22.757 --> 01:00:24.940
and nativity is such that,

01:00:24.940 --> 01:00:26.920
the folks who are directly impacted

01:00:26.920 --> 01:00:29.980
are leading the work of whatever that is.

01:00:29.980 --> 01:00:31.340
So when we're doing organizing

01:00:31.340 --> 01:00:33.986
about working conditions in academia.

01:00:33.986 --> 01:00:35.480
That's us right here.

01:00:35.480 --> 01:00:37.030
We're doing that.

01:00:37.030 --> 01:00:40.373
And when we're doing organizing around,

01:00:41.710 --> 01:00:44.593
the hyper surveillance and
policing of welfare provision,

01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:49.040
even if we may have been on WIC before,

01:00:49.040 --> 01:00:50.300
if you're not on WIC right now,

01:00:50.300 --> 01:00:51.750
you need to be in a listening role,

01:00:51.750 --> 01:00:56.660
you need to be in a supporting
and amplifying role.

01:00:56.660 --> 01:00:58.890
And so for me, that's why I
ask about who is the "we,"

01:00:58.890 --> 01:01:00.960
'cause usually I'm not the "we,"

01:01:00.960 --> 01:01:04.923
and it's my job to really
remind myself that, so.

01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:11.257
- Yeah, that's such an
important point, Savannah.

01:01:11.257 --> 01:01:13.103
And I think it's also,

01:01:15.960 --> 01:01:20.537
where I might start to think
about Christen's question

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:26.070
about community and the university.

01:01:26.070 --> 01:01:29.570
I mean, I think it's an
impossible relationship,

01:01:29.570 --> 01:01:33.110
especially on a campus like
Penn and like many others

01:01:33.110 --> 01:01:35.660
that has just continually expanded

01:01:35.660 --> 01:01:39.060
to the detriment of the
surrounding community.

01:01:39.060 --> 01:01:40.853
And there's constantly, and is,

01:01:41.730 --> 01:01:46.730
this week, organizing around
building that's happening,

01:01:49.760 --> 01:01:53.570
on one particular end of the campus.

01:01:53.570 --> 01:01:58.570
So, we can understand
Penn as a growing space

01:02:00.380 --> 01:02:02.080
that is dispossessing people

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:05.080
with a long history of
dispossessing people.

01:02:05.080 --> 01:02:09.130
And honestly, even this
building that I'm in,

01:02:09.130 --> 01:02:11.660
which is the anthropology department,

01:02:11.660 --> 01:02:16.530
which is located in the Penn Museum,

01:02:16.530 --> 01:02:18.860
which was built on

01:02:19.920 --> 01:02:24.627
the former site of the
Philadelphia Blockley Almshouse.

01:02:25.780 --> 01:02:28.540
Across the street is Franklin Field,

01:02:28.540 --> 01:02:31.870
which was the potters
field for the Almshouse.

01:02:31.870 --> 01:02:34.950
Again, dug up and...

01:02:34.950 --> 01:02:38.360
in order to create the buildings
that are currently here

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:40.300
and in fact, for those of you

01:02:40.300 --> 01:02:43.630
who know some of the recent
history of revelations

01:02:43.630 --> 01:02:45.500
around what's going on in the Penn Museum,

01:02:45.500 --> 01:02:48.090
I mean, some of the crania

01:02:48.090 --> 01:02:50.560
that ended up in Morton's Collection

01:02:50.560 --> 01:02:52.830
come from the Blockley Almshouse

01:02:52.830 --> 01:02:54.393
and our Black Philadelphians.

01:02:55.560 --> 01:03:00.420
So, these are the histories we
inhabit that I inhabit daily.

01:03:00.420 --> 01:03:02.300
I walk into this building,

01:03:02.300 --> 01:03:04.490
I am here with the people
living in the building

01:03:04.490 --> 01:03:06.670
with the ancestors living in the building.

01:03:06.670 --> 01:03:10.160
And on top of the people
whose graves were destroyed

01:03:10.160 --> 01:03:12.360
in order to create the building.

01:03:12.360 --> 01:03:13.193
So,

01:03:14.560 --> 01:03:18.200
community building in
this university space

01:03:19.733 --> 01:03:24.733
is impossible, really like
theoretically impossible.

01:03:26.410 --> 01:03:27.560
That said,

01:03:27.560 --> 01:03:28.620
again,

01:03:28.620 --> 01:03:31.830
with the view toward
redistributing the resources

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:36.323
of the institution beyond its walls.

01:03:38.250 --> 01:03:42.400
I mean, I think the arts is really a way

01:03:42.400 --> 01:03:44.500
in which at least I know how to do that.

01:03:44.500 --> 01:03:47.550
Other people know how to do
it through other mechanisms,

01:03:47.550 --> 01:03:49.470
but since that is my background,

01:03:49.470 --> 01:03:51.690
that is the tact that I take.

01:03:51.690 --> 01:03:54.920
And it's always a negotiation

01:03:54.920 --> 01:03:59.920
and an ongoing struggle of
recognizing the assumptions

01:04:03.340 --> 01:04:04.750
that you have and the moments

01:04:04.750 --> 01:04:06.940
when you think everybody's
on the same page.

01:04:06.940 --> 01:04:09.120
And then you realize that you're not,

01:04:09.120 --> 01:04:11.730
and oh, maybe we should have
had a conversation about this,

01:04:11.730 --> 01:04:12.563
or I wouldn't have thought

01:04:12.563 --> 01:04:14.510
to have a conversation about this,

01:04:14.510 --> 01:04:18.410
or it's going on right now in a good way,

01:04:18.410 --> 01:04:20.210
in a productive and generative way

01:04:20.210 --> 01:04:23.680
with the collaboration that
Grace Sanders Johnson and I have

01:04:25.070 --> 01:04:26.840
on a course that we're
team teaching right now

01:04:26.840 --> 01:04:28.320
called "Citing Black Girlhood."

01:04:28.320 --> 01:04:30.860
And one of our community partners

01:04:30.860 --> 01:04:32.750
is the Colored Girls Museum in Germantown.

01:04:32.750 --> 01:04:36.930
That's run by my very old
friend, Vashti DuBois.

01:04:36.930 --> 01:04:40.580
And, there (indistinct)
have just been moments

01:04:40.580 --> 01:04:42.410
when we have to regroup and say,

01:04:42.410 --> 01:04:45.490
wait, what did you mean
when you said that?

01:04:45.490 --> 01:04:49.470
Or how is this going to work
when we travel to Jamaica

01:04:49.470 --> 01:04:52.150
or when we travel to South Africa,

01:04:52.150 --> 01:04:54.100
how are the portraits going to go with us?

01:04:54.100 --> 01:04:57.090
How are the other portraits
going to join that exhibit?

01:04:57.090 --> 01:04:58.863
There are all of these things that,

01:05:00.040 --> 01:05:02.070
we each may have particular ideas

01:05:02.070 --> 01:05:03.920
about the logistics surrounding them,

01:05:03.920 --> 01:05:05.370
but the logistics come out

01:05:06.320 --> 01:05:09.724
of a theoretical and experiential miras.

01:05:09.724 --> 01:05:12.550
So, they're all things to
be constantly negotiated.

01:05:12.550 --> 01:05:14.640
So I think I would just echo Savannah

01:05:14.640 --> 01:05:17.360
in talking about listening

01:05:17.360 --> 01:05:19.900
and inhabiting the listening role,

01:05:19.900 --> 01:05:22.403
yes, as Christen just wrote in the chat.

01:05:23.820 --> 01:05:28.820
But I would also say that
I think all of these words,

01:05:28.910 --> 01:05:32.410
abolishing, decolonizing, liberating

01:05:32.410 --> 01:05:35.890
have temporalities also,

01:05:35.890 --> 01:05:37.790
I think there are times

01:05:37.790 --> 01:05:42.000
when we do the Weberian politics,

01:05:42.000 --> 01:05:44.950
the hard, boring of hard boards.

01:05:44.950 --> 01:05:46.650
And there are other times

01:05:46.650 --> 01:05:49.430
when it is the moment to burn it down,

01:05:49.430 --> 01:05:52.573
and we have to be able to recognize,

01:05:54.822 --> 01:05:57.180
when is which time,

01:05:57.180 --> 01:06:01.110
when to just say, you know
what, this isn't going to work.

01:06:01.110 --> 01:06:03.950
We're not going to be able
to go forward in that way

01:06:03.950 --> 01:06:05.800
and actually torch it,

01:06:05.800 --> 01:06:08.840
and then move in a different
direction or regroup

01:06:08.840 --> 01:06:10.930
come up from the ashes and think again

01:06:10.930 --> 01:06:13.420
about what we want to build.

01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:16.370
We can't beat our heads
against the same wall forever.

01:06:16.370 --> 01:06:20.013
We have to build the new
wall and see if it holds.

01:06:20.910 --> 01:06:23.490
- Mm, thank you so much for that.

01:06:23.490 --> 01:06:25.640
All of you, I'm looking at the time.

01:06:25.640 --> 01:06:26.850
And I think what I'd like to do

01:06:26.850 --> 01:06:29.120
is go to some of the questions in the chat

01:06:29.120 --> 01:06:30.110
and I'm gonna combine,

01:06:30.110 --> 01:06:33.980
there's two questions that
were directly messaged to me,

01:06:33.980 --> 01:06:35.210
and I think they're related.

01:06:35.210 --> 01:06:37.550
So, is it cool with y'all if
I read both of the questions

01:06:37.550 --> 01:06:42.110
and then we can see if you
can answer them together.

01:06:42.110 --> 01:06:43.860
And I think you might have touched on

01:06:43.860 --> 01:06:46.320
some of what is being
asked in the question.

01:06:46.320 --> 01:06:48.170
So you could just maybe amplify

01:06:48.170 --> 01:06:49.620
just a little bit of
what you've already said,

01:06:49.620 --> 01:06:50.773
but the first one,

01:06:52.470 --> 01:06:55.400
how does care, love and tenderness

01:06:55.400 --> 01:06:57.160
come into y'all's respective work?

01:06:57.160 --> 01:06:59.100
I'm thinking about Bell Hooks,

01:06:59.100 --> 01:07:02.580
King's Beloved Community
and Cornel West's work.

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:04.020
Aimee, you started our conversation

01:07:04.020 --> 01:07:05.990
expressing the care that
you have for our speakers.

01:07:05.990 --> 01:07:09.660
I'm wondering what practices
you take in your daily lives

01:07:09.660 --> 01:07:14.090
that cultivate spaces
or communities of care.

01:07:14.090 --> 01:07:15.587
All right, so that's one question, right?

01:07:15.587 --> 01:07:17.530
And I think, tell me
if I'm stretching here

01:07:17.530 --> 01:07:20.190
to try to combine these.

01:07:20.190 --> 01:07:22.650
How can we keep the sacredness and rituals

01:07:22.650 --> 01:07:25.670
by the people we are working
with and learning from,

01:07:25.670 --> 01:07:29.290
without having to adopt a
colonial supremacist method

01:07:29.290 --> 01:07:33.550
of the, in quotes, "need to
know or need to explain,"

01:07:33.550 --> 01:07:36.300
can something just be
sacred or unexplainable,

01:07:36.300 --> 01:07:38.360
can part of our method be embedded

01:07:38.360 --> 01:07:40.333
in the mystery, in our work?

01:07:42.670 --> 01:07:45.150
And so, I throw those
two questions out for you

01:07:45.150 --> 01:07:47.700
to answer them individually

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:49.763
or how you might see them connected.

01:07:53.350 --> 01:07:55.513
- Maybe I'll start, (murmurs) okay?

01:07:57.760 --> 01:07:59.250
Definitely opacity,

01:07:59.250 --> 01:08:02.570
I think has to be a guiding principle

01:08:02.570 --> 01:08:04.040
of anthropological work forward.

01:08:04.040 --> 01:08:05.580
And there are many people

01:08:05.580 --> 01:08:10.130
who have moved toward that
in different kinds of ways,

01:08:10.130 --> 01:08:14.360
and ethnographies that are models.

01:08:14.360 --> 01:08:15.840
I think of that kind of work,

01:08:15.840 --> 01:08:19.103
I think Ana Lara's work,

01:08:20.740 --> 01:08:23.140
Black freedom: Queer sovereignty,

01:08:23.140 --> 01:08:25.067
Black sovereignty: Queer freedom

01:08:27.230 --> 01:08:30.840
is one example of that kind of work.

01:08:30.840 --> 01:08:32.643
And I think that is care work,

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:37.970
the respect for the right to not be known,

01:08:37.970 --> 01:08:41.140
to not be parsed, to not
be described to death

01:08:42.170 --> 01:08:44.400
and still in relation.

01:08:44.400 --> 01:08:48.060
I think that that is
definitely part of a practice

01:08:48.060 --> 01:08:51.680
of care within a research process.

01:08:51.680 --> 01:08:54.860
I think also for me,

01:08:54.860 --> 01:08:59.860
in terms of practices of
care in life generally,

01:08:59.920 --> 01:09:03.063
or in the academic life,

01:09:03.980 --> 01:09:06.070
has to do with gathering

01:09:06.070 --> 01:09:08.453
and creating the conditions for gathering

01:09:09.290 --> 01:09:14.290
in ways that can offer support and love

01:09:16.110 --> 01:09:20.940
and really a view of the whole person

01:09:20.940 --> 01:09:24.290
and to also acknowledge
one's own vulnerability

01:09:24.290 --> 01:09:26.200
and need for support and love

01:09:26.200 --> 01:09:29.410
and the need to be seen as a whole person.

01:09:29.410 --> 01:09:32.770
And part of that is also about mentoring

01:09:32.770 --> 01:09:37.770
and trying to make sure
there are more and more,

01:09:40.760 --> 01:09:44.030
and exponentially more Black women

01:09:44.030 --> 01:09:48.280
who are at the stage where
they're able to enact

01:09:49.500 --> 01:09:51.253
their true vision.

01:09:52.610 --> 01:09:54.920
I think Black women constitute...

01:09:54.920 --> 01:09:57.430
Christina, actually Sharpe
told me this one time

01:09:57.430 --> 01:09:59.600
when I was agonizing over

01:09:59.600 --> 01:10:02.003
how many tenure letters
I had to write one year,

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:07.820
she told me that 2% of full
professors are Black women.

01:10:07.820 --> 01:10:10.080
And that's why we get asked to write

01:10:10.080 --> 01:10:14.260
for everybody in every discipline.

01:10:14.260 --> 01:10:18.520
And I think, one can
see that and feel that

01:10:18.520 --> 01:10:20.950
as a burden of course,

01:10:20.950 --> 01:10:25.840
but if one sees it instead as
always enlarging the community

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:30.760
and in always being in a
condition of learning together

01:10:30.760 --> 01:10:35.480
and embracing the work of others

01:10:36.980 --> 01:10:39.110
in a fulsome way

01:10:39.110 --> 01:10:42.663
then I think that too is
part of a practice of caring.

01:10:45.670 --> 01:10:47.580
- Did Savannah or Christen,

01:10:47.580 --> 01:10:51.470
do you wanna address
either of the questions

01:10:51.470 --> 01:10:54.216
or there's another-
- Oh, go ahead.

01:10:54.216 --> 01:10:55.049
There's (indistinct).
- We both said the same thing

01:10:55.049 --> 01:10:56.040
at same, go ahead.

01:10:56.040 --> 01:10:57.110
- Oh, no.

01:10:57.110 --> 01:10:58.600
I (indistinct) mine is brief.

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:00.700
I mean, obviously
everything that Deb said,

01:11:00.700 --> 01:11:05.370
and it's interesting that
you talked about the mystery

01:11:05.370 --> 01:11:07.270
of our work, Aimee,

01:11:07.270 --> 01:11:09.760
because I think for me,

01:11:09.760 --> 01:11:13.090
one of the ways of
interrupting these kind of...

01:11:13.090 --> 01:11:14.540
I'm not sure this is
exactly what you're asking,

01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:18.380
but kind of this colonial
effect of extraction

01:11:19.240 --> 01:11:22.023
in our workspaces is part
of what you're asking about,

01:11:22.023 --> 01:11:27.023
like how do we not rehearse
that when we're in community?

01:11:27.560 --> 01:11:29.980
I think rather than mystery,

01:11:29.980 --> 01:11:32.616
one of the things that I've
really looked to is transparency

01:11:32.616 --> 01:11:33.450
(indistinct) of the inverse.

01:11:33.450 --> 01:11:36.910
So you might be a mystery
when it shows up on JSTOR.

01:11:36.910 --> 01:11:40.160
But I think in the work of
having more transparency,

01:11:40.160 --> 01:11:42.060
which I think initially
I thought was about like,

01:11:42.060 --> 01:11:44.540
oh, sharing the writing,
see what you think about,

01:11:44.540 --> 01:11:48.800
but actually really being
transparent about the labor.

01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:51.373
Like what is the thing
that my job is like,

01:11:52.670 --> 01:11:53.683
how do you get paid?

01:11:55.494 --> 01:11:56.660
What do you need to get out of this

01:11:56.660 --> 01:11:57.640
in order to keep your job,

01:11:57.640 --> 01:12:02.250
like, be very clear about the CV

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.440
and the pace of productivity,

01:12:05.440 --> 01:12:06.460
all this other kind of things

01:12:06.460 --> 01:12:08.587
that I think are really
submerged in our work.

01:12:08.587 --> 01:12:12.000
And we never talk about it in
the pieces that we're writing.

01:12:12.000 --> 01:12:14.670
And we also rarely talk about
it with a community, right?

01:12:14.670 --> 01:12:17.640
But then as opposed to being...

01:12:17.640 --> 01:12:20.570
I think conversations,
I thought that initially

01:12:20.570 --> 01:12:24.550
were about like, oh, do
you support this project?

01:12:24.550 --> 01:12:26.600
So even when I was doing my dissertation,

01:12:28.160 --> 01:12:29.980
the kinds of relationships
that I had with folks...

01:12:29.980 --> 01:12:32.170
I knew some people already,
I had some relationships

01:12:32.170 --> 01:12:35.120
'cause I worked at the
school for several years.

01:12:35.120 --> 01:12:38.120
And when folks participated
in the research,

01:12:38.120 --> 01:12:38.953
it wasn't because they were like,

01:12:38.953 --> 01:12:41.060
we wanna contribute to
anthropological knowledge.

01:12:41.060 --> 01:12:43.640
It was like, oh, Ms. Shange
is going back to school.

01:12:43.640 --> 01:12:45.380
Ms. Shange wants to become a professor.

01:12:45.380 --> 01:12:48.124
This is her dream, let's
help her everybody.

01:12:48.124 --> 01:12:48.957
And people would be like,

01:12:48.957 --> 01:12:50.020
this is so good, what you're doing, baby.

01:12:50.020 --> 01:12:51.730
We love you, how can we help you, baby?

01:12:51.730 --> 01:12:53.630
This is so wonderful.

01:12:53.630 --> 01:12:54.980
And it wasn't because they were like,

01:12:54.980 --> 01:12:56.870
you're gonna be a great
anthropologist of our community.

01:12:56.870 --> 01:12:59.710
They were like, we see
that you want something.

01:12:59.710 --> 01:13:01.440
And there's something
that I can share with you

01:13:01.440 --> 01:13:02.970
that helps you get to where you wanna go.

01:13:02.970 --> 01:13:04.290
And that was their gift to me.

01:13:04.290 --> 01:13:06.170
So it wasn't that I was
kind of bringing back

01:13:06.170 --> 01:13:07.680
some kind of gift to community.

01:13:07.680 --> 01:13:08.513
It was really them saying,

01:13:08.513 --> 01:13:10.920
you wanna do this cool thing,
you wanna be a professor,

01:13:10.920 --> 01:13:12.010
we support you.

01:13:12.010 --> 01:13:14.620
I gotta sign this thing and
tell you a story, I got that.

01:13:14.620 --> 01:13:17.300
And so that for me, was
a gift from community.

01:13:17.300 --> 01:13:18.600
And I think that kind of transparency

01:13:18.600 --> 01:13:21.690
moving forward into the work
that I'm doing now to be like,

01:13:21.690 --> 01:13:24.940
hey, I need to write an
article in the next two years.

01:13:24.940 --> 01:13:26.883
It can really be about anything.

01:13:28.190 --> 01:13:29.950
Like the space that
we're sharing right now.

01:13:29.950 --> 01:13:32.150
So the project that I'm
working on right now

01:13:33.150 --> 01:13:35.300
in terms of the political
work I was sharing before

01:13:35.300 --> 01:13:36.610
talking with the folks,
and then being like,

01:13:36.610 --> 01:13:39.750
Hmm, well, I really wanted
to work on writing too.

01:13:39.750 --> 01:13:41.220
I'm actually a poet.

01:13:41.220 --> 01:13:42.470
Is that something we could do together?

01:13:42.470 --> 01:13:45.750
So now, we're in a very
kind of slow form process

01:13:45.750 --> 01:13:47.490
of writing together,

01:13:47.490 --> 01:13:49.650
what we're thinking of
as a collective history

01:13:49.650 --> 01:13:53.660
of the process of producing
the George Floyd Resolution.

01:13:53.660 --> 01:13:56.080
Which might at some point
show up in an academic journal

01:13:56.080 --> 01:13:57.350
or some version of that.

01:13:57.350 --> 01:13:59.390
But really it was because of being able

01:13:59.390 --> 01:14:02.980
to have this conversation about my labor,

01:14:02.980 --> 01:14:04.310
and then also being able to be like,

01:14:04.310 --> 01:14:07.200
this does not have to
enter this space at all.

01:14:07.200 --> 01:14:08.760
We don't have to do any of that.

01:14:08.760 --> 01:14:10.680
And then I will go and write

01:14:12.474 --> 01:14:14.310
another piece about another thing.

01:14:14.310 --> 01:14:16.380
And so I think that transparency

01:14:16.380 --> 01:14:19.670
has been a part of being able to hold care

01:14:19.670 --> 01:14:23.600
in terms of the kind of
nuts and bolts labor aspect

01:14:23.600 --> 01:14:24.863
of producing scholarship.

01:14:25.720 --> 01:14:29.640
- Mm, I love that so much.

01:14:29.640 --> 01:14:33.880
And Savannah does nothing
I've ever seen in any textbook

01:14:35.260 --> 01:14:37.550
on activist research methods.

01:14:37.550 --> 01:14:40.060
The transparency question is
never about what you just said.

01:14:40.060 --> 01:14:42.500
which really would just change everything

01:14:42.500 --> 01:14:45.020
in terms of the relationships
that would be developed.

01:14:45.020 --> 01:14:47.030
And it's like, well, I reveal that,

01:14:47.030 --> 01:14:48.870
how I'm gonna use the research on the IRB

01:14:48.870 --> 01:14:50.520
and signing these forms of consent,

01:14:50.520 --> 01:14:53.050
but to be really honest about the capital

01:14:53.050 --> 01:14:55.000
that is gained for you as a researcher

01:14:55.000 --> 01:14:56.723
in the work and what that means.

01:14:57.820 --> 01:14:59.070
Thank you for that.

01:14:59.070 --> 01:15:01.280
I'm wondering if, I'm looking...

01:15:01.280 --> 01:15:02.240
I'm gonna wait for people

01:15:02.240 --> 01:15:03.620
to add a little bit more to the chat.

01:15:03.620 --> 01:15:06.390
There's one more question
that was privately sent to me,

01:15:06.390 --> 01:15:08.230
but I think you've kind
of already answered it,

01:15:08.230 --> 01:15:11.510
but I wanted to see, because
Christen asked a question

01:15:11.510 --> 01:15:13.570
and I'm one wondering if Deb or Savannah,

01:15:13.570 --> 01:15:18.570
you have a question for
your interlocutors here.

01:15:18.720 --> 01:15:20.560
- Can I say something
about the healing thing,

01:15:20.560 --> 01:15:23.640
really (indistinct) quickly.
- Sure, yes.

01:15:23.640 --> 01:15:26.600
- I mean about the care,

01:15:26.600 --> 01:15:29.470
because I wanted to add
this question of healing

01:15:30.440 --> 01:15:33.060
because at least for me,

01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:36.963
part of what I've really
tried to do in my work.

01:15:37.990 --> 01:15:40.190
And when I say work, I mean
that really expansively,

01:15:40.190 --> 01:15:41.990
not just in the research topic,

01:15:41.990 --> 01:15:45.140
but also just kind of how
I engage with the academy

01:15:45.140 --> 01:15:48.130
and how I engage
intellectually, et cetera,

01:15:48.130 --> 01:15:50.603
is creating spaces of healing for people,

01:15:51.790 --> 01:15:53.720
which is about community
building, et cetera.

01:15:53.720 --> 01:15:57.253
But it's also about caring
for the sacred, right.

01:15:58.423 --> 01:16:03.423
And caring for people's spiritual selves.

01:16:07.492 --> 01:16:09.010
And so I wanted to put that out there

01:16:09.010 --> 01:16:11.030
because I think that that's something

01:16:11.030 --> 01:16:13.060
that also goes unsaid

01:16:13.060 --> 01:16:15.770
and people don't
necessarily talk about that.

01:16:15.770 --> 01:16:16.707
But I mean,

01:16:18.060 --> 01:16:20.000
in a lot of ways,

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:25.000
what we do can come back
to giving people the space

01:16:26.560 --> 01:16:27.750
and the time to heal

01:16:28.990 --> 01:16:31.320
in ways they may not have been otherwise

01:16:31.320 --> 01:16:35.470
because of the neoliberal
insistence on production.

01:16:35.470 --> 01:16:39.880
And so, sometimes we
just create enough space

01:16:39.880 --> 01:16:42.230
for somebody to just have a moment

01:16:42.230 --> 01:16:44.190
where they're actually
thinking about themselves

01:16:44.190 --> 01:16:45.560
and thinking about their lives

01:16:45.560 --> 01:16:47.760
and thinking about what
they've been through

01:16:49.010 --> 01:16:51.313
in a way that's protected and safe.

01:16:53.500 --> 01:16:54.333
- Hmm.

01:16:55.920 --> 01:16:56.753
Yes.

01:16:59.680 --> 01:17:01.570
Savannah or Deb, do you have a question

01:17:01.570 --> 01:17:06.403
for this collective, this trio?

01:17:12.880 --> 01:17:13.833
If not, I can ask-

01:17:15.200 --> 01:17:16.700
- I mean I have nothing but questions,

01:17:16.700 --> 01:17:18.620
but I just like this piece about,

01:17:18.620 --> 01:17:20.690
is it great but is it anthropology

01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:24.670
is wild to hear from you as the person

01:17:24.670 --> 01:17:26.800
who literally made me
into an anthropologist,

01:17:26.800 --> 01:17:28.300
like out of thin air (laughs),

01:17:31.430 --> 01:17:36.000
because I have no anthropology degrees

01:17:37.320 --> 01:17:39.690
and now have this anthropology position,

01:17:39.690 --> 01:17:43.210
I think because I was an
apprentice to you in this work,

01:17:43.210 --> 01:17:45.634
I think in a really real
way and an old time version

01:17:45.634 --> 01:17:47.780
of how it means to come into a profession,

01:17:47.780 --> 01:17:49.100
but just kind of reflecting on this,

01:17:49.100 --> 01:17:50.920
is it great but is anthropology.

01:17:50.920 --> 01:17:54.900
I was also being asked by people

01:17:54.900 --> 01:17:57.263
in great positions of
power in our discipline,

01:17:58.962 --> 01:18:00.380
to get the job that I have right now.

01:18:00.380 --> 01:18:02.320
What makes you one of us?

01:18:02.320 --> 01:18:04.200
Like really like tests of faith,

01:18:04.200 --> 01:18:06.550
this kind of test of
faith around anthropology,

01:18:08.840 --> 01:18:11.958
it's just wild that it doesn't
stop to (indistinct) that.

01:18:11.958 --> 01:18:15.660
- (indistinct) I'm not
(laughs), and I don't mean that.

01:18:15.660 --> 01:18:19.733
- I did not say that is
how I got this position.

01:18:24.960 --> 01:18:27.950
Pro's tip, if anyone
here gets that question,

01:18:27.950 --> 01:18:29.513
answer ethnographically.

01:18:30.670 --> 01:18:31.563
Works every time.

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:35.307
- Yeah, so I'm looking at the time

01:18:35.307 --> 01:18:39.000
and I'm thinking that
we can move to closing.

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:41.163
So like, what do you wanna leave us with?

01:18:44.767 --> 01:18:46.740
I also had a question about
teaching, but I'll save it,

01:18:46.740 --> 01:18:48.180
but just what is...

01:18:48.180 --> 01:18:49.340
There's so much to think with here.

01:18:49.340 --> 01:18:52.830
I mean, we could go on for
two more hours at least,

01:18:52.830 --> 01:18:55.790
but what is the thing you
want to leave us with,

01:18:55.790 --> 01:18:57.020
or maybe it was that,

01:18:57.020 --> 01:18:59.430
I love that idea of the burst of light.

01:18:59.430 --> 01:19:01.220
And I think you put it
in the chat, Savannah,

01:19:01.220 --> 01:19:03.310
that when we're thinking
about those five nodes

01:19:03.310 --> 01:19:06.120
or those five tenets, really
the stars and the nice sky,

01:19:06.120 --> 01:19:07.580
the burst of light,

01:19:07.580 --> 01:19:09.530
and maybe there's
something that has come up

01:19:09.530 --> 01:19:12.630
in this conversation today that is another

01:19:12.630 --> 01:19:14.020
star in that constellation

01:19:14.020 --> 01:19:17.113
that we wanna add to this dark night sky.

01:19:18.528 --> 01:19:22.430
But yeah, can I start with you, Christen

01:19:22.430 --> 01:19:24.990
to close this out with
what you would want us to

01:19:24.990 --> 01:19:26.173
continue to think with?

01:19:28.290 --> 01:19:30.420
- That's a great question.

01:19:30.420 --> 01:19:31.680
You said a burst of light

01:19:31.680 --> 01:19:33.807
and all I could think
about was Audre Lorde's,

01:19:33.807 --> 01:19:34.997
"A Burst of Light,"

01:19:36.746 --> 01:19:40.223
and really thinking about,

01:19:41.140 --> 01:19:42.760
what Savannah was talking about

01:19:42.760 --> 01:19:46.620
in terms of stars and constellations,

01:19:46.620 --> 01:19:48.860
which to me, bursts of light and stars,

01:19:48.860 --> 01:19:51.153
my brain goes together with those things.

01:19:52.300 --> 01:19:53.820
And so, I mean, for me,

01:19:53.820 --> 01:19:56.143
I think I would probably,

01:20:01.177 --> 01:20:06.177
leave us with the thought that
in order for us to be able

01:20:06.260 --> 01:20:11.260
to really get to a point
where our work is moving us

01:20:13.980 --> 01:20:18.980
in a direction that feels
like it ensures life.

01:20:20.427 --> 01:20:23.310
And it is freeing, right?

01:20:23.310 --> 01:20:26.530
'Cause freedom is a word
we haven't really used yet,

01:20:26.530 --> 01:20:28.540
but it's embedded in this question

01:20:28.540 --> 01:20:29.850
of abolition and liberation.

01:20:29.850 --> 01:20:34.850
I think that we have to keep
searching for new alternatives

01:20:38.310 --> 01:20:42.070
and be comfortable with our discomfort.

01:20:42.070 --> 01:20:45.470
I think that that to
me is really important.

01:20:45.470 --> 01:20:46.870
Meaning we cannot get to the point

01:20:46.870 --> 01:20:49.320
where we're so comfortable in where we are

01:20:49.320 --> 01:20:53.800
that we neglect to remember
the need for constant struggle

01:20:53.800 --> 01:20:55.300
and for vigilance.

01:20:55.300 --> 01:20:56.550
And I think to me,

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:59.713
that is something that
I wanna leave us with.

01:21:02.260 --> 01:21:03.173
- Thank you.

01:21:04.320 --> 01:21:07.160
- Okay, vigilance, oh Deb, go ahead.

01:21:07.160 --> 01:21:08.920
- Savannah, did you wanna go next?

01:21:08.920 --> 01:21:12.105
- Yes, 'cause I think Deb
should have the last word.

01:21:12.105 --> 01:21:13.897
- Stop.
- (indistinct), did it.

01:21:13.897 --> 01:21:16.980
- Deb has incredibly mentored everyone

01:21:16.980 --> 01:21:19.510
on this call.
- Everyone.

01:21:19.510 --> 01:21:22.120
Where are the flower emojis?

01:21:22.120 --> 01:21:24.010
Put the flower emojis in the chat

01:21:24.010 --> 01:21:27.520
because Deb deserves the
flower now, not no roses.

01:21:27.520 --> 01:21:29.030
I want blue orchids, bougainvillea,

01:21:29.030 --> 01:21:32.283
okay, something with a little
flavor, a little movement.

01:21:33.790 --> 01:21:36.390
But I think this is gonna sound...

01:21:36.390 --> 01:21:38.410
I don't mean this in
(indistinct) like a...

01:21:38.410 --> 01:21:39.243
What's that word grandiose way.

01:21:39.243 --> 01:21:41.920
But you know that saying
that folks will ask

01:21:41.920 --> 01:21:44.060
in certain kinds of community spaces,

01:21:44.060 --> 01:21:46.633
what kind of ancestor do you wanna be?

01:21:47.531 --> 01:21:50.340
And thinking about that
as a orientation to

01:21:52.470 --> 01:21:53.950
political work to life,

01:21:53.950 --> 01:21:57.400
certainly to like family
inheritance, right.

01:21:57.400 --> 01:22:01.070
I think it's also applicable
within anthropology.

01:22:01.070 --> 01:22:04.110
In terms of what is
the table we're setting

01:22:04.110 --> 01:22:06.980
for the meal that we're not
ever gonna be able to eat.

01:22:06.980 --> 01:22:08.880
And I think, Deb, I really
heard you speaking to this.

01:22:08.880 --> 01:22:10.250
And again like you...

01:22:11.432 --> 01:22:13.280
This may not be the time of the space,

01:22:13.280 --> 01:22:16.730
but like the levels to which
you have really created,

01:22:16.730 --> 01:22:20.040
infrastructure for life and survival

01:22:20.040 --> 01:22:22.710
in a place that was not designed for us

01:22:22.710 --> 01:22:24.940
and not for yourself.

01:22:24.940 --> 01:22:27.110
Tunnels, pathways, bunkers.

01:22:27.110 --> 01:22:31.420
You are like a real general in this war.

01:22:31.420 --> 01:22:32.253
And in ways, (indistinct),

01:22:32.253 --> 01:22:34.237
has always said specifically about you.

01:22:34.237 --> 01:22:35.870
So I just wanna lift that up.

01:22:35.870 --> 01:22:39.570
But like when folks are writing about...

01:22:39.570 --> 01:22:41.470
Like, think about in 2016,

01:22:41.470 --> 01:22:43.280
somebody's doing a
review of this and that,

01:22:43.280 --> 01:22:44.510
like, what is the thing

01:22:44.510 --> 01:22:48.570
that you want to be right
before your little parentheses

01:22:48.570 --> 01:22:50.540
and not in terms of your theory.

01:22:50.540 --> 01:22:51.530
But what is it terms of,

01:22:51.530 --> 01:22:53.850
how are you positioning
yourself in this genealogy?

01:22:53.850 --> 01:22:54.733
Are you offering something

01:22:54.733 --> 01:22:56.833
that might be worth something to somebody?

01:22:58.730 --> 01:23:01.750
And I think that's also true
in terms of our teaching,

01:23:01.750 --> 01:23:02.990
in terms of our family legacies,

01:23:02.990 --> 01:23:04.260
in terms of our political work.

01:23:04.260 --> 01:23:07.360
Because I don't...

01:23:07.360 --> 01:23:09.270
Maybe, listen, prove me wrong.

01:23:09.270 --> 01:23:11.700
I don't think that I'm gonna live to see

01:23:11.700 --> 01:23:13.200
what I think of as liberation.

01:23:14.650 --> 01:23:17.220
And I also feel like I'm responsible

01:23:17.220 --> 01:23:20.240
for offering something that
might be of use to the person

01:23:20.240 --> 01:23:23.940
who is gonna be there at
that moment and at that day,

01:23:23.940 --> 01:23:25.490
and it's not gonna be
something big, right.

01:23:25.490 --> 01:23:27.020
It's gonna be something small.

01:23:27.020 --> 01:23:30.650
But I think that for me is
how I orient towards this.

01:23:30.650 --> 01:23:32.980
And similarly, you talked about teaching.

01:23:32.980 --> 01:23:34.940
I would love to hear
your teaching question,

01:23:34.940 --> 01:23:38.730
but in classes, I hope the
thing that the students get

01:23:38.730 --> 01:23:41.150
is not the content, right?

01:23:41.150 --> 01:23:44.060
Not the quotation in the book,

01:23:44.060 --> 01:23:45.620
but something they can take with them

01:23:45.620 --> 01:23:47.850
and put in their pocket and
fondle up between their fingers

01:23:47.850 --> 01:23:49.293
on a really hard day.

01:23:50.860 --> 01:23:52.440
- Yeah, that was-
- I'm sorry.

01:23:52.440 --> 01:23:53.627
I was just tapping in the chat.

01:23:53.627 --> 01:23:55.500
Yes, Savannah, that's totally
how I think about teaching.

01:23:55.500 --> 01:23:57.480
And then I always feel
like I'm doing it wrong.

01:23:57.480 --> 01:24:00.223
I'm like, this is about your spirit.

01:24:01.750 --> 01:24:04.160
Anyway, Deb,

01:24:04.160 --> 01:24:05.230
take us home.
- Yeah,

01:24:05.230 --> 01:24:07.223
I was just gonna say that,

01:24:08.070 --> 01:24:12.420
and this is on my mind
because graduate students

01:24:12.420 --> 01:24:14.910
and a couple junior faculty

01:24:14.910 --> 01:24:19.420
have raised this question recently,

01:24:19.420 --> 01:24:22.230
they have a thing that
they're passionate about

01:24:22.230 --> 01:24:24.783
that they think they should wait to do,

01:24:28.071 --> 01:24:29.830
until the dissertation is done

01:24:29.830 --> 01:24:32.540
or until I get tenure or until...

01:24:33.832 --> 01:24:38.220
And I think what is important

01:24:38.220 --> 01:24:42.853
is to do the thing one
is passionate about now

01:24:43.690 --> 01:24:47.270
and figure out how to
work it into the thing

01:24:47.270 --> 01:24:49.690
that's going to finish a dissertation

01:24:49.690 --> 01:24:52.320
or get tenure or whatever,

01:24:52.320 --> 01:24:57.320
because if we do not live
our lives, our dreams,

01:24:59.310 --> 01:25:01.870
if we do not bring our whole selves,

01:25:01.870 --> 01:25:04.210
but instead try to contort constantly

01:25:04.210 --> 01:25:07.180
into whatever the figure is

01:25:07.180 --> 01:25:09.960
that we imagine people want to see

01:25:09.960 --> 01:25:13.070
from us in these spaces,

01:25:13.070 --> 01:25:15.663
then the spaces themselves
will never change.

01:25:16.570 --> 01:25:21.120
And one does one's own
mental health a disservice

01:25:21.120 --> 01:25:26.120
to not pursue the important
question, the creative writing,

01:25:27.550 --> 01:25:31.320
the activism, the organizing,

01:25:31.320 --> 01:25:33.730
all of the above together, whatever it is.

01:25:33.730 --> 01:25:36.170
So, I guess what I would say is

01:25:37.200 --> 01:25:40.780
the true transformation can only come,

01:25:40.780 --> 01:25:44.900
not only because we inhabit
these rarefied spaces,

01:25:44.900 --> 01:25:49.250
but because we inhabit them as
the total people that we are

01:25:49.250 --> 01:25:51.300
with the desires that we have,

01:25:51.300 --> 01:25:53.640
and that we bring everybody with us

01:25:53.640 --> 01:25:55.283
into those spaces as well.

01:25:59.600 --> 01:26:00.433
- Oh, thank...

01:26:00.433 --> 01:26:01.540
I was writing in the chat.

01:26:01.540 --> 01:26:03.620
So my teaching question was,
you all already answered it.

01:26:03.620 --> 01:26:06.633
I was just wondering
how we've talked about,

01:26:07.700 --> 01:26:09.580
terminology and how we think about

01:26:09.580 --> 01:26:12.530
and across institutional
spaces, but what does it mean,

01:26:12.530 --> 01:26:14.875
not just in our own research,
but in the classroom,

01:26:14.875 --> 01:26:17.843
how do we teach, are we
doing that when we teach?

01:26:19.086 --> 01:26:20.430
And I don't know who said that about,

01:26:20.430 --> 01:26:23.180
it's not about the quote,
it's the (laughs)...

01:26:23.180 --> 01:26:24.740
What can you take with you

01:26:25.760 --> 01:26:28.040
to move through these streets?

01:26:28.040 --> 01:26:29.313
Ugh, anyway.

01:26:30.480 --> 01:26:33.120
I just wanna say before we log off

01:26:33.120 --> 01:26:35.100
that we have speaking of teaching

01:26:35.100 --> 01:26:37.910
and speaking of the next
generation and mentorship

01:26:37.910 --> 01:26:40.160
that we have with us in our company,

01:26:40.160 --> 01:26:44.090
some of the newly
admitted graduate students

01:26:44.090 --> 01:26:45.760
in African American Studies.

01:26:45.760 --> 01:26:48.980
And so, they're here
listening to this conversation

01:26:48.980 --> 01:26:50.830
so glad to have you all here,

01:26:50.830 --> 01:26:52.283
so glad you could make it.

01:26:53.310 --> 01:26:57.173
I have nothing but the deepest adoration

01:26:58.830 --> 01:27:00.780
for all three of you.

01:27:00.780 --> 01:27:05.780
And the work that you do
has liberated my ability

01:27:06.200 --> 01:27:08.330
to be in the academy.

01:27:08.330 --> 01:27:09.763
And I mean that sincerely.

01:27:11.634 --> 01:27:13.070
From the first time I met you, Deb,

01:27:13.070 --> 01:27:16.380
I realized that it was possible
to be in the academy...

01:27:16.380 --> 01:27:18.500
What is it to be in it, but not of it.

01:27:18.500 --> 01:27:20.480
To be in it, but not of it, right.

01:27:20.480 --> 01:27:23.040
And to find that way, I think
June Jordan talks about this,

01:27:23.040 --> 01:27:25.530
that you're moving through spaces,

01:27:25.530 --> 01:27:28.660
like an eel almost, without
getting anything on you,

01:27:28.660 --> 01:27:30.160
but as you're moving through spaces,

01:27:30.160 --> 01:27:31.630
you're creating those tunnels

01:27:31.630 --> 01:27:32.910
that Savannah was talking about

01:27:32.910 --> 01:27:34.910
for others to come through behind you.

01:27:34.910 --> 01:27:37.890
So maybe they don't have
to be so slippery (laughs).

01:27:37.890 --> 01:27:39.200
Maybe they can be upright

01:27:39.200 --> 01:27:41.083
as they move through those tunnels.

01:27:42.000 --> 01:27:46.290
So anyway, I just wanna thank
everyone for coming out.

01:27:46.290 --> 01:27:47.280
I am rem...

01:27:47.280 --> 01:27:51.170
Please forgive me, we have
another Endeavors Session,

01:27:51.170 --> 01:27:53.690
I believe on the 20...

01:27:53.690 --> 01:27:55.310
Let me look at my...

01:27:55.310 --> 01:27:56.950
We'll send you all the notification,

01:27:56.950 --> 01:28:00.820
but we'll be discussing
Eli Anderson's latest book,

01:28:00.820 --> 01:28:01.990
which will be very exciting.

01:28:01.990 --> 01:28:06.270
It'll be myself, Eli
Anderson and Gerald Jaynes.

01:28:06.270 --> 01:28:07.920
So I hope you all can make that.

01:28:07.920 --> 01:28:08.830
And I'm gonna look on...

01:28:08.830 --> 01:28:10.993
I believe it's the 21st,

01:28:16.210 --> 01:28:18.300
but you'll get a notification around that.

01:28:18.300 --> 01:28:21.010
But I just wanna thank
you so much, Christen,

01:28:21.010 --> 01:28:22.770
Savannah and Deb,

01:28:22.770 --> 01:28:25.630
and for all of your wonderful questions,

01:28:25.630 --> 01:28:28.300
this was just like, just the bomb

01:28:28.300 --> 01:28:31.710
that we all need in these times.

01:28:31.710 --> 01:28:33.770
And today, I mean that this week,

01:28:33.770 --> 01:28:38.070
like I needed this so desperately.

01:28:38.070 --> 01:28:39.610
Take good care of yourselves.

01:28:39.610 --> 01:28:42.060
I'll be speaking to you soon, I'm sure.

01:28:42.060 --> 01:28:44.180
Thank you all for coming out

01:28:44.180 --> 01:28:47.423
and see you soon and next time.

